window.WIKI_ENTRY={"id": "concept-099", "name_zh": "梦", "name_en": "dreams", "tagline": "赛斯说的「梦」不是你睡着了才出现的副产品，而是你一直在其中的正常状态：梦里你并没有离开，只是把注意力的焦点从物质世界挪开；梦不是被压抑的愿望清单，而是一场创造行动，里面的事和醒时一样真实，身体照单全收；信息可以带回来（只是被转译过），你也可以主动跟它打交道。", "explanation": "这一页是「梦」标签的主页，讲的是做梦这件事本身：你在梦里处于什么状态、梦在替你做什么、你能拿它怎么办。赛斯的主轴有五条：做梦是每个意识不可或缺的正常状态，关不掉也不停；梦里你并没有离开身体或世界，只是把注意力的焦点从物质刺激挪开（第 531 节、第 149 节、第 174 节）；梦不是被压抑愿望的伪装清单，而是一次次创造行动，做梦的知性比你们的计算机还快（第 341 节、第 889 节、第 795 节）；梦里的行动和醒时一样真实，会以电子实相被解码、铭印进细胞并改变身体（第 187 节、第 126 节、第 722 节）；梦的信息带得回来，但带回的是转译过、被各层潜意识各自解读过的版本，所以记不住不等于没起作用（第 570 节、第 93 节、第 88 节）。其余各块分别处理可能性试演、恶梦与疗愈梦、梦里遇到的他人与集体梦、睡前的暗示与练习，以及梦与语言、文明的关系。\n\n分工：这一页不展开梦所处的那个宇宙或场的结构，那是 concept-005「梦宇宙」；梦自己如何生成空间，见 concept-100「梦空间」；直接经验而非象征的那一类根源梦，见 concept-101「根源梦」。这里只在你正在做梦这条线上把总账说清楚。", "definitions": [{"zh": "这个梦活动似乎与意识状态本身分离，而且比你们物质系统里的意识状态先来到。你们无法中止梦活动，它自己也永不会停止。", "en": "This dream activity seems divorced from the conscious state itself, and came before the conscious state within your physical system. It cannot be turned off, and it will not cease.", "source": "Session 196"}, {"zh": "同时，你还将各种资料向其他居住于完全不同世界中的‌“你”传达，相对的，你也由它们那边接受了相当分量的讯息。你并不会和醒时的那个‌“我”失去联系，你只不过是转移了注意力，不再把焦点放在他身上罢了。如果你从另外这个角度来看那个世间的‌“我”，不必说——你会认为白天那个肉体的‌“我”和现在我所说的这个状态的‌“我”一样奇怪。不过这种说法并不能完全成立，因为这个睡梦中的‌“我”比起那个自鸣得意的世间的‌“我”在知识上不知高出来多少倍。", "en": "At the same time, you make this information available to all these other portions of your identity, who dwell in entirely different realities, and you receive from them comparable information. You do not lose contact with your ordinary waking self. You simply do not focus upon it. You turn your attention away. In the daytime you simply reverse the process. If you were looking at your daily normal self from the other viewpoint, you see, using an analogy here, you might find that physically waking self as strange as you now find the sleeping self. The analogy will not hold however, simply because this sleeping self of yours is far more knowledgeable than the waking self of which you are so proud.", "source": "Session 531"}, {"zh": "每个梦都是一种创造的行为，无法以其他方式启动，具有高度的个别性。", "en": "Every dream is a creative act that could be initiated in no other way, highly individual.", "source": "Session 341"}], "blocks": [{"id": "B01", "title": "睡着的不是你：梦里你并没有离开，只是换了注意力的焦点", "note": "这一页的总前提。赛斯先把睡着了就等于你不在了这个常识拆掉：「让我在此强调，你们在睡眠中绝对不是无意识的」（第 531 节）——之所以你以为无意识，只是你们的规则让你第二天早上记不起来。同一个意思他说得更具体：「你并不会和醒时的那个“我”失去联系，你只不过是转移了注意力，不再把焦点放在他身上罢了。」（第 531 节）他还补了一句反转：如果你从梦里那个角度看白天那个肉体的自己，你会觉得它同样奇怪，而且「这个睡梦中的“我”比起那个自鸣得意的世间的“我”在知识上不知高出来多少倍」。\n\n焦点这个词是理解整条线索的钥匙。第 149 节说，做梦的人参与梦经验时从来不觉得虚幻，只有人格离开梦经验再回头想看，才觉得不真实，因为那时注意力和能量的焦点又回到物质宇宙——「所以说，实相是能量与注意力聚焦的结果」。第 153 节把界线一句抹平：「外显的界线不是界线，只是注意力的焦点有别而已。」第 94 节、第 175 节接着说内我持续不断改变焦点，意识只不过是内我在特定时间聚焦的方向，焦点从物质实相转到其他实相，才产生层次上的改变。\n\n因此梦不等于睡觉。第 174 节直说「你不一定要睡觉才能做梦，每个人都做过白日梦，这时你可以更清楚看到焦点的转换」；第 173 节连赛斯自己也下场：「我的确会做梦，但不一定是睡觉时做。」第 257 节他甚至否认自己按你们的说法在做梦——他只是带着有意识的意图转移注意力焦点，跟你们从一个国家走到另一个国家差不多。第 219 节、第 282 节、第 721 节把结论收成三句话：自己在梦里并不受物质感知的局限；梦中意识某种程度上独立于物质系统运作，但连结没有断；你本来就活在一个「清醒与做梦的精神环境」里，在两个环境里你都是有意识的。\n\n最后给初学者一个不吓人的说法：两边互相记不住，是节律而不是缺陷。第 244 节：「如果你在清醒状态中对梦地点没什么记忆，那就记住，当你处于梦状态时，你对醒时地点也一样没什么记忆。两者都是合理的，也都是实相。」第 170 节形容那种处境——做梦的人没法向你证明门外那条街存在，也没法证明他睡的那张床是物质的，因为他的注意力被导向了另一个频率。第 329 节反过来问：你睡着时比坐在教室里听我声音时还要清醒，你的梦在你醒时的自己看来是幻相，那我要不要告诉你，你醒时的经验在做梦的自己看来是怎样？（第 329 节）", "src": "早期课第4册第 149、153、170、173、174、175 节；早期课第3册第 94 节；早期课第5册第 219 节；早期课第6册第 244、257 节；早期课第7册第 282、329 节；《时空之外》第 531 节；《未知的实相卷二》第 721 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 149 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 153 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 170 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 173 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 174 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 175 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 94 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 219 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 244 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 257 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 282 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 329 节"}, {"n": 13, "id": null, "label": "第 531 节"}, {"n": 14, "id": null, "label": "第 721 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "你从来没有变成无意识", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1842", "zh": "让我在此强调，你们在睡眠中绝对不是无意识的。你们之所以会认为睡眠是一种无意识状态是因为你们的“规则”如此，使得你们第二天早上完全记不起来睡梦中的情景。当然有些人会在某种程度上知道有过一些活动，也有办法能记得一些这类的活动。", "en": "Now let me emphasize here that you are simply not unconscious. It only seems that you are, because as a rule you remember none of this in the morning. To some extent, however, some people are aware of these activities, and there are also methods that will enable you to recall them to some degree.", "source": "时空之外 · 第531节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "531", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1844", "zh": "同时，你还将各种资料向其他居住于完全不同世界中的“你”传达，相对的，你也由它们那边接受了相当分量的讯息。你并不会和醒时的那个“我”失去联系，你只不过是转移了注意力，不再把焦点放在他身上罢了。如果你从另外这个角度来看那个世间的“我”，不必说——你会认为白天那个肉体的“我”和现在我所说的这个状态的“我”一样奇怪。不过这种说法并不能完全成立，因为这个睡梦中的“我”比起那个自鸣得意的世间的“我”在知识上不知高出来多少倍。", "en": "At the same time, you make this information available to all these other portions of your identity, who dwell in entirely different realities, and you receive from them comparable information. You do not lose contact with your ordinary waking self. You simply do not focus upon it. You turn your attention away. In the daytime you simply reverse the process. If you were looking at your daily normal self from the other viewpoint, you see, using an analogy here, you might find that physically waking self as strange as you now find the sleeping self. The analogy will not hold however, simply because this sleeping self of yours is far more knowledgeable than the waking self of which you are so proud.", "source": "时空之外 · 第531节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "531", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2187", "zh": "可是，你活在一个清醒与做梦的精神环境里。在两个环境里你都是有意识的。", "en": "You live in a waking and dreaming mental environment, however. In both environments you are conscious.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第721节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "721", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1292", "zh": "在梦中，意识某种程度上独立运作于物质系统之外。在投射中，这种独立更为显著。当然在肉体死亡时，意识与物质之间的连结中断了。它中断了，但是没有完全切断。", "en": "In dreams consciousness operates to some degree independently of the physical system. In projections this independence is more pronounced. In physical death of course the connection between consciousness and matter is broken. It is broken and yet not entirely severed.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第282节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "282", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0997", "zh": "但自己A并不局限在自我的感知里，因此可以说，自己A的感知并不完全的局限在他所存在的领域。在梦或其他的状态里就没有如此的局限了，当意识还在这些其他的状况时，自己A也仍存在于物质实相里。", "en": "But self A is not limited to the egos perceptions only, therefore it may be said that self As perceptions are not limited, in toto, to the field in which it exists. For it is not so limited in dreams and in other states, yet while consciousness is in these other conditions, self A still exists within physical reality.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第219节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "219", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "界线只是焦点的差别", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0516", "zh": "做梦的人在参与梦经验时，不会觉得虚幻不实，那时候感觉经验是真的，而且有些梦的确比醒时经验更为鲜明生动。只有在人格离开梦经验或梦宇宙后再去回想时，才可能感觉梦经验不真实，因为这时候的注意力和能量的焦点又再度回到物质宇宙中。所以说，实相是能量与注意力聚焦的结果。", "en": "The feeling of unreality is not felt when the dream experience is being participated in by the dreamer. At that time the experience is felt to be real, and some dreams indeed are more vivid than waking experience. It is only when the personality passes out of the dream experience, or the dream universe, that the dream experience in retrospect may appear unreal. For now, again, the focus of attention and energy is in the physical universe. Reality then is a result of the focus of energy and attention.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第149节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "149", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0533", "zh": "我经常说，所有这些区分或分隔都是武断的，全部都是一个存在于另一个之内。外显的界线不是界线，只是注意力的焦点有别而已。甚至这个内在自我从这一刻到另一刻都不是同一个，因为它不是一个静止的东西，而是连续行动的一部分。不过，内在自我对潜意识、梦宇宙和内我，远比外在自我熟悉多了。", "en": "I have often said that all these divisions and separations are arbitrary. All exist one within another. Apparent boundaries are not boundaries, but only differences in the focus of attention. Even this inner ego is not the same from one given moment to another, for it is not a static thing, but is a part of continuing action. It is much more familiar with the subconscious and with the dream universe, and with the inner self, than it is with the outside ego, however.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第153节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "153", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0614", "zh": "学习改变你自己意识焦点的经验极有帮助，它甚至可以提升你们梦的助益价值。你不一定要睡觉才能做梦，每个人都做过白日梦，这时你可以更清楚看到焦点的转换。我之前就想提这件事，鲁柏可以请求做漂浮梦（levitation dreams），他将会做这种梦。", "en": "The experience involved in learning to change your own focus of awareness will be most beneficial. It will even add to the beneficial nature of your own dreams. You do not have to sleep to dream. Every individual has had his daydreams, and here you can see more clearly this change of focus. I meant to mention this earlier. Ruburt can request levitation dreams, and he will have them.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第174节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "174", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0635", "zh": "无论如何，这并不涉及焦点深度上的改变。当一个人将焦点从物质实相转换到其他实相，才会全然产生不同层次的改变。梦境里显然我们就有这种变化的一个例子。为什么在梦境中才可能有这种情形，而醒时则没有？其主要原因就是生存需求通常已经获得满足。", "en": "This does not involve however a change of focus in depth. A change to a different level entirely occurs when the individual switches focus from physical reality to other realities. In the dream state obviously we have an example of such change. One of the main reasons why this is possible within the dream state, rather than in the waking state, is that survival necessities have usually been satisfied.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第175节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "175", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0251", "zh": "催眠是检视梦的一种方法。我们的方法或许是最好的一个。内我持续不断改变它的焦点。我说过意识只不过是内我在特定时间聚焦的方向而已。为了检视梦的实相，必须趁自我没有把它的范围单单限制在伪装实相之内的时候，改变内我的焦点，转到内我在其中移动的那些方向。", "en": "Hypnosis is one method of examining dreams. Our method is perhaps the best one. The inner self constantly changes its focus. I have said that consciousness is merely the direction in which the inner self focuses at any particular time. In order to examine the reality of dreams it is necessary to change the focus of the inner self to those directions in which the inner self moves, when the ego does not limit its scope to camouflage reality only.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第94节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "94", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0577", "zh": "在梦境里，做梦的人不可能证明他熟悉的大门外那熟悉的街道是存在的，他的注意力已暂时被导向一个不同频的实相那边去，平时在窗外的树木对他而言不复存在。很难去要求一个做梦的人，证明他所睡的那张床，或是他头旁边的床头柜是物质实相，或是证明放置床铺的木头地板确实存在。这真的非常困难，因为对做梦的人来说，这类物体并不存在。", "en": "Now. In the dream state it would be impossible for the dreamer to prove the existence of the familiar street outside of his familiar door. His attention is momentarily directed toward a different sort of reality. The ordinary trees outside of his window do not exist for him. It would be highly difficult to ask a man while he dreamed to prove the physical reality of the bed in which he slept, or the bedside table which was at his head, or to prove the existence of the wooden floor upon which the bed rested. Highly difficult indeed, for such objects do not exist for our dreamer.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第170节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "170", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1122", "zh": "现在。我没有梦见鲁柏，因为按照你们做梦的这种说法，我根本就不做梦。可以说，我是带着有意识的意图，转移我的注意力焦点。我把自己的所有部分全都放在任何特定的意识状态中，而且我进入种种意识状态的方式，在某方面和你们可以从一个实质国家移动到另一个实质国家是相同的。", "en": "Now. I do not dream of Ruburt because I do not dream at all, in the same terms that you do. I switch the focus of my attention with conscious intent, so to speak. I take all portions of myself into any particular state of consciousness, and I enter various states of consciousness somewhat in the same manner that you can move from one physical country to another.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第257节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "257", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0609", "zh": "我的确会做梦，但不一定是睡觉时做。我可以意识到那些在我人格心理架构里面出现的行动。理论上人类人格甚至可以，或可能在醒时状态下意识到梦。不过实际上来讲，这不见得是好事，而且似乎也不会发生。", "en": "I do dream, but not necessarily while in a sleeping state. I am conscious of these actions that occur within the psychological framework of my personality. Theoretically the human personality can, or could, be conscious of dreams even while he was in a waking state. Practically however this does not appear to be of benefit, nor does it seem to occur.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第173节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "173", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "两个方向互相记不住，也互相打量", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1430", "zh": "在某些方面，你们睡着时比坐在你们的教室里听我的声音时还要清醒。你的梦在你平常醒时的自己看来是幻相。我要不要告诉你，你正常醒时的经验在你做梦的自己看来是怎样？我要不要告诉你，这两个实相哪一个更有效，哪一个的扭曲最少？我要不要告诉你，你的能力究竟是从哪里来的？", "en": "You are, in some respects, more awake when you sleep than you are when you sit in your classroom and listen to my voice. Your dreams appear as illusion to your normal waking self. Shall I tell you how your normal waking experiences appear to your dreaming self? Shall I tell you which of the two realities are more valid and which is least distorted? Shall I tell you where your abilities do originate?", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第329节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "329", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1431", "zh": "你认为你的能力源自于坐在教室里的自己吗？你的内在本体与帮你刷牙的自己有什么关系？可是，当你刷牙时，你肯定很清醒；当你做梦时，你肯定会说你正在睡觉，你的意识死了。", "en": "Do you think your abilities originate with the self that sits in the classroom? What does your inner identity have to do with the self that brushes your teeth? Yet surely you are wide awake when you brush your teeth, and when you dream surely you will say you are sleeping and your consciousness is dead.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第329节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "329", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1079", "zh": "现在。如果你在清醒状态中对梦地点没什么记忆，那就记住，当你处于梦状态时，你对醒时地点也一样没什么记忆。两者都是合理的，也都是实相。", "en": "Now. If you have little memory of your dream locations while you are in the waking state, then remember you have as little memory of waking locations when you are in the dream state. Both are legitimate, and both are realities.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第244节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "244", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B02", "title": "梦关不掉：它是意识的正常运作，从胎儿到动物都在做", "note": "第二块讲停不下来。第 196 节给了一句很硬的判词：那个内在的做梦情境「对于每个意识、人类或其他物种而言，这种做梦情境不可或缺」，接着是「你们无法中止梦活动，它自己也永不会停止」——唯一会被注意到的时刻，只是人格有意识的部分暂时离开物质刺激。第 109 节说得更日常：你们自动建构梦，用的是你们存在的一个层次，不管醒着睡着、觉察不觉察，它「和你们的呼吸或走路一样自动执行」。第 196 节又补：就算有意识的自己正忙于日常琐事，心也照样持续在做梦；第 202 节称这是一个「连续的过程」，第 472 节说这类活动不论醒着睡着都在继续，还深度参与梦意象与梦构造的形成。\n\n所以「不记得」不等于「没做」。第 13 节一上来就点出你们的窘境：连自己昨夜梦的片段都无法有意识地记住。第 24 节接着提醒，你不能把一个梦按在特定的钟表时间里——它不按你的时间表走。真正的代价被第 795 节说穿了：任何抑制活动的东西也会抑制你的梦，「任何人被剥夺了足够的做梦时间，极有可能开始在醒时产生幻象，因为已积累了太多的经验需要处理」，许多次要的荷尔蒙活动只在梦境发生，细胞成长与恢复活力也在身体睡眠时加速。第 794 节解释为什么梦回想起来总接不上头：梦经验是以一种醒时脑子无法处理的复杂性发生的。第 744 节顺手指出，把正常的醒时意识模式织进睡眠与做梦状态、形成一个整合的新模式，本来就不必靠迷路来完成。\n\n梦的节律也写在身体的作息里。《灵魂永生》第 532、533 节讨论改变睡眠模式会怎样同时改进健康、让梦中的象征更清晰而不至于在漫长的睡眠里被忘掉；那里提到的「分段睡眠」是为了少丢一些有实际价值的创造性资料。\n\n最后一组是把梦从成年人晚上才会的事还原成一切生命的底子：第 194 节说小孩子做梦频率更高、内容更生动，因为他们不像大人那样确认物质环境，要更常回到接近清醒的阶段去检查；第 793 节说儿童的梦比成人更强烈，因为「脑子正在练习它形成事件的活动」，婴儿在梦里做出超过目前身体能力的动作。第 912 节更往前推：「胎儿会做梦」，意识的成形被基因性的梦所伸展，在脑子完全成形之前，观念形成的能力已经在运作。同册第 912 节把这些称为先天的梦，说它们在你一生中以某种形式持续着。《健康之道》第 1 节则从反面举例：有些存在在清醒与睡眠之间交替，睡眠时身体过程慢下来、不变老得那么快，而做梦的精神过程并不减缓。", "src": "早期课第1册第 13、24 节；早期课第3册第 109 节；早期课第4册第 194、196 节；早期课第5册第 202 节；早期课第9册第 472 节；《灵魂永生》第 532、533 节；《未知的实相卷二》第 744 节；《心灵的本质》第 793、794、795 节；《梦、进化与价值完成》第 912 节；《健康之道》第 1 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 13 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 24 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 109 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 194 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 196 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 202 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 472 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 532 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 533 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 744 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 793 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 794 节"}, {"n": 13, "id": null, "label": "第 795 节"}, {"n": 14, "id": null, "label": "第 912 节"}, {"n": 15, "id": null, "label": "第 1 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "它和呼吸一样自动，且永不停止", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0796", "zh": "这些不知来自何处但清晰可见的灵感的确来自某处，这个某处就是那个内在的做梦情境，对于每个意识、人类或其他物种而言，这种做梦情境不可或缺。这里我用意识一词，涵盖全部有觉知（awareness）的生物。这个梦活动似乎与意识状态本身分离，而且比你们物质系统里的意识状态先来到。你们无法中止梦活动，它自己也永不会停止。一般而言，唯一要注意的就是，人格有意识的部分何时会与物质刺激大幅脱离。", "en": "These apparent inspirations from nowhere definitely come from somewhere, and this somewhere is that inner dreaming condition, which is a necessity on the part of every consciousness, human or otherwise. Here I am using the word consciousness to cover all organisms with awareness. This dream activity seems divorced from the conscious state itself, and came before the conscious state within your physical system. It cannot be turned off, and it will not cease. It will only be noticed, as a rule, when the aware portions of the personality are disconnected from their physical stimulations to some considerable degree.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第196节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "196", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0362", "zh": "你们应该明白，你们对这部影片的觉察并不表示影片只是在你们出现时才开始。可以这么说，你们自动建构梦，大部分用的是你们存在的一个层次，不管你们醒着或睡着，也不管你们有没有觉察到梦，都是如此。这是一种内在的象征操纵，和你们的呼吸或走路一样自动执行，这是你们自身物质和心灵架构、你们的化学和电磁构造的一个副产品。", "en": "You would know better than to think that your awareness of the movie signified that the movie began only when you appeared. You construct dreams automatically, so to speak, with one level of your being in the main, whether you wake or sleep, and whether you are aware of the dreams or not. This is an inner symbol manipulation that is carried out as automatically as you breathe or walk, a by-product of your own physical and psychic structure, of your chemical and electromagnetic constitution.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第109节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "109", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0791", "zh": "他无意识的沟通也是遵循这样的模式，这当然包括他的梦。大家应该记得很清楚，梦甚至可以存在于清醒状态的个休，即便他自己并不知道。就算是有意识的自己正忙于日常琐事，他的心也一样会持续做梦的这个活动。", "en": "His unconscious communications will also follow this same pattern. This of course includes his dreams. It may be well to remember here that dreams exist even for an individual in the waking state, though he is not aware of them. His mind continues this activity even while the conscious self goes about its daily chores.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第196节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "196", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0792", "zh": "有意识的自我不认得这些梦，但内我不会忘了要记录下来，因此梦得以存在，而且会形成自己的电磁管道（electromagnetic channel）, 借此影响肉体本身。不管自我是否意识到这些刺激，肉体组织经常受到所有的刺激而改变，不管科学界是否了解到这些特定的刺激存在与否，肉体组织仍会受到这些刺激影响。因此，不管个体是否处于清醒或睡眠状态，肉体组织在连续的梦经验形成的过程当中，都会对这个无意识且持续不断的精神活动起反应。所有这些实相进而形成了人格，而且除非能完全了解这些实相，否则这个人格本身仍是一个谜团。", "en": "These dreams go unrecognized by the conscious ego; but they do not go unrecorded by the inner self, and they therefore exist, and they form electromagnetic channels of their own to which—make that by which—the physical body itself is affected. The physical organism is constantly changed by all stimuli, whether or not the ego is aware of the stimuli. And it is affected by stimuli, whether or not science realizes that the particular stimuli even exists. Therefore, the physical organism reacts to this continuous unconscious Mental activity in the form of continuing dream experiences, whether or not the individual is awake or asleep. All of these realities go into the formation of the personality, and unless they are all understood the personality itself will remain a mystery.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第196节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "196", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0870", "zh": "我曾告诉你们，不管自我是否醒着，也不管它是否保留了梦的内容，梦是一个连续的过程。所以不管自我是醒着或睡着，星光自己的旅行都常常发生。", "en": "I have told you that dreams are a continuing process, whether or not the ego wakes or sleeps, and whether or not it has, or retains, any knowledge of the dreaming. So also the astral self journeys often, whether the ego wakes or sleeps.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第202节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "202", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1582", "zh": "这类行为自然不论你们醒着还是睡着都在继续，并且深度参与着梦的意象与梦的构造的形成。有些活动领域你们并不反应，只因为你们尚未在自己里面激活相感应的路径。这些领域需要你们付出多得多的努力，但即便是物质大脑，里面也潜伏着许多种活动的可能性，而这些活动至今仍大体不为人知。", "en": "This sort of behavior continues of course whether you are waking or sleeping, and is highly involved in the construction of dream images and constructions . There are areas of activity to which you do not react, simply because you have not activated sympathetic pathways within yourself. These require much more effort on your part, but even the physical brain has latent within it the possibilities for many kinds of activities that still remain generally unknown.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第472节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "472", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0010", "zh": "比如，在你们的层面上，甚至对自己做的梦，都无法有意识地记忆其中的片断。也正是因为这样，即使在特意保有意识的情况下，你们对某个概念的记忆也几乎无法维持到下个礼拜。你们所知的“自我”根本不可能在这一世（at this time）维持有意识的支配地位。（停顿）", "en": "You on your plane for example do not even have conscious memory of your own dream fragments. For that matter you can hardly remember one idea from one week to another on a conscious deliberate basis. It is simply impossible for the ego as you know it to maintain conscious dominance at this time. （Pause.）", "source": "早期课第1册 · 第13节", "year": "1963–64", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第1册", "session": "13", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0040", "zh": "你绝对不能强迫它们存在于一段特定的钟表时间，也不能对一个梦做同样的事。虽然，在这个阶段，听起来似乎是不可能的，不过，你在清醒状态下控制这种资讯的能力，比控制一个梦更好。这里你为了全我的目的，学着使用意识的某些部分，但这件事我以后再讨论。", "en": "You can never force them to exist for a specified period of clock time, nor for that matter can you do that to a dream. Though this may sound impossible at this stage, nevertheless you can control such data when in a waking state better than you can control a dream. Here you learn to use certain parts of the consciousness for the purposes of the whole self, but I will discuss this at a later date.", "source": "早期课第1册 · 第24节", "year": "1963–64", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第1册", "session": "24", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "剥夺做梦是有代价的", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2522", "zh": "非常少量的酒精也有用。任何抑制活动的东西也将抑制你的梦。众所周知，任何人被剥夺了足够的做梦时间，极有可能开始在醒时产生幻象，因为已积累了太多的经验需要处理。有许多次要的荷尔蒙活动只在梦境——而不在任何其它时间——发生。甚至细胞成长和重获活力，也在身体睡眠时加速。", "en": "A very small amount of alcohol can also serve. Anything that suppresses activity will also suppress your dreams. As is known, anyone deprived of sufficient dreaming will most likely begin to hallucinate while in the waking state, for too much experience has built up that needs processing. There are many secondary hormonal activities that take place in the dream state and at no other time. Even cellular growth and revitalization are accelerated while the body sleeps.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第795节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "795", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2488", "zh": "不过，在做梦时，通常由脑子投射而借身体行动加强的完全的感官图片（sense-picture）是不必要的。那些梦的经验在早晨的后顾或回想时，常像是接不了头或焦点模糊，只因它们以平常醒时的脑子无法处理的一种复杂性发生。", "en": "In dreaming, however, the full sense-picture usually projected by the brain, and reinforced by bodily action, is not necessary. Those dream experiences often seem out of joint or out of focus in morning’s hindsight, or in retrospect, simply because they occur with a complexity that the brain could not handle in ordinary waking terms.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第794节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "794", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2253", "zh": "在睡眠里，你们所了解的普通脑波，录下没被正常处理过的一个混乱的经验丛林。生物上或心灵上，这种迷失方向感并没有必要。正常的醒时意识及它特有的模式的确可以跟到睡眠里，结果会产生一个混合的脑波。你们所认为的意识在这种情况不会扩展得非常厉害。你会跟随你自己延续性与理解的模式，把它织入睡眠与做梦状态，形成一个胜利的组合了所有模式的一个“新的”模式，就如在我们的课里多少发生了的。", "en": "In sleep your ordinary brain waves as you understand them register a chaotic jungle of experience not normally processed. Biologically or psychically, there is little need for such disorientation. The normal waking consciousness, with its characteristic patterns, can indeed follow [into sleep]. A mixture of brain waves would result. Consciousness as you think of it expands tremendously under such conditions. You would follow your own pattern of continuity and understanding, weaving this into the sleep and dream states, forming a “new” pattern that triumphantly combines all, as to some extent this occurs in our sessions.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第744节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "744", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1695", "zh": "自己之间的分界就不会那么的严格，肉体与精神的工作会轻松一些，而身体本身有固定的时间能获得休息与更新。不像现在，不管它的情况如何，它都必须至少等上十六个小时左右，才有得休息。因为其他在作梦状态中与化学反应有关的理由，改变了睡眠模式之后，身体的健康也会跟着改进。这特定的时间表对患精神分裂症的人也会有帮助，一般而言对有沮丧问题的人，或那些精神不安定的人也会有帮助。", "en": "The divisions between the self would not be nearly as severe. Physical and mental work would be easier, and the body itself would gain steady periods of refreshment and rest. Now, as a rule, it must wait, regardless of its condition, at least for some sixteen hours. For other reasons having to do with the chemical reactions during the dream state, bodily health would be improved; and this particular schedule would also be of help in schizophrenia, and generally aid persons with problems of depression, or those with mental instability.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第532节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "532", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1705", "zh": "结果产生了一个仿佛的二元性，以及自己的一部分对另一部分的不信任。在这个过程中，失去了许多有实际价值的创造性资料。前所提及的分段睡眠法更容易得到这些资料，而醒时的自己会更觉得神清气爽。梦中的象征会显得更清晰，而不在你现在这么长的睡眠时间里被忘掉。", "en": "A seeming duality is the result and a mistrust on one part of the self toward the other. Much creative material of quite practical value is lost in the process. The procedures mentioned would allow much greater access to such information, and the waking self would be more refreshed. The symbolism in dreams would appear with greater clarity, not, for example, be lost through the many hours you now give to sleep.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第533节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "533", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "孩子、胎儿、以及别的活着的方式", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0777", "zh": "小孩子做梦的频率比较高，内容也比较生动。不过，由于他们对于物质环境不像大人那么确认，所以为了检视物质环境状况，他们更常回到接近清醒的阶段。就小孩子的梦中行动来说，处在深层睡眠阶段时，他们会漫游得更远。", "en": "Children dream more vividly and more often. They return more frequently however to periods of near wakefulness, in order to check their physical environment, since they are not as sure of it as adults are. In deep periods of sleep children range further away, as far as their dream activities are concerned.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第194节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "194", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2470", "zh": "儿童的梦比成人的要更强烈，因为脑子正在练习它形成事件的活动。在某些身体的能力能被发动之前，这些必须先发展。婴儿在梦中玩耍，做出超过他们目前的身体能力的动作。虽然外在的刺激很重要，但梦的游戏的内在刺激甚至更重要。", "en": "Children’s dreams are more intense than those of adults because the brain is practicing its event-forming activities. These must be developed before certain physical faculties can be activated. Infants play in their dreams, performing physical actions beyond their present physical capacities. While external stimuli are highly important, the inner stimuli of dream play are even more so.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第793节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "793", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2778", "zh": "胎儿会做梦。当其肉体的生长在子宫里发生的时候，其意识的成形也被基因性的梦所伸展。这些特殊的胎儿取向的梦是最难描述的，因为它们真的涉及了个人意识的轮廓线之形成。这种梦提供了主观的理解，而思维由之发展。以那种说法，在脑子本身完全成形之前，完整的思想就是可能的。就是思维的过程有助于将脑子带入活动，而非其反面（全都相当热切的）。", "en": "The fetus dreams. As its physical growth takes place in the womb, so the shaping of its consciousness is also extended by genetic dreams. These particular fetus-oriented dreams are most difficult to describe, for they are actually involved with forming the contours of the individual consciousness. Such dreams provide (pause) the subjective understanding from which thoughts are developed, and in those terms complete thoughts are possible before the brain itself is fully formed. It is the process of thinking that helps bring the brain into activity, and not the other way around (all quite intently).", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第912节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "912", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2960", "zh": "不过，除此之外，他们的精力是以不同于你们的方式被利用的。他们在清醒与睡眠状态之间交替（停顿良久），而当他们在睡眠时，不会变老得那么快。他们的身体过程慢了下来。虽然这是真的，他们做梦的精神过程却并没减缓。在梦境有个大得多的沟通，因此，有些功课是在梦里教授的，同时其它的则在醒时状态进行。", "en": "Beside this, however, their energy was utilized in a different fashion than yours is. They alternated between the waking and dream states (long pause), and while asleep they did not age as quickly. Their bodily processes slowed. Although this was true, their dreaming mental processes did not slow down. There was a much greater communication in the dream state, so that some lessons were taught during dreams, while others were taught in the waking condition.", "source": "健康之道 · 第1节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "1", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B03", "title": "梦不是愿望的满足，是一次创造行动", "note": "赛斯在这一块把梦等于被压抑欲望的伪装这条解释线正面推回去，同时也不接受梦是没脑子杂音这种看法。他要说的核心在《早期课》第 341 节那一句：「每个梦都是一种创造的行为，无法以其他方式启动，具有高度的个别性。」同一层意思在晚期说得更细：第 795 节——「梦涉及了高度的创造行为」，它们不只直觉地建立，还是以「远超过你对逻辑的概念的逻辑」形成的，创造性行为再透过联想过程极精确地拼合成梦的事件；做这些事的那个自己，「以惊人的辨识力工作，筛检数据，把一些送到身体的某部分，并构成记忆」。\n\n对初学者最实用的一句是《梦、进化与价值完成》第 889 节的强调：「梦世界不是个漫无目的、非逻辑或不具知性的活动场」，只是你们自己的视野把它广大实相的大部分关掉了，因为「在做梦的知性可胜过你们的计算机」；你们的思考能力之所以是你们认识的样子，恰恰是因为做梦的自己不受阻断地用上了知性与直觉合起来的全部力量。第 790 节则提醒，一般梦研究对梦的游戏性和创造性估计得太低——孩子在花园里扮恶鬼，晚餐铃一响就回到安全宇宙，梦常常就是这么用来面对恐惧的。\n\n至于为什么梦看起来荒诞：第 23 节说，人在睡眠中以「更真实而且比较不扭曲的方式」创造，醒时的物质世界更多是做梦的自己创造的产物；第 57 节补一句「没有一个心灵行动是静止的。它也不是了无生气」，梦世界只是在你们这个层面没有物质实相；第 183 节说明内在经验在梦里不受时间障碍，因此有多余的心灵空间留给其他情感经验；第 196 节则指出这种无意识参与梦的活动，确实与你们所谓的灵感或创造性活动相关——梦经验常常改变人类的发展方向。第 259 节把责任交回你手上：如果你创造自己的梦，那么你也创造自己的客观环境，而且你像操纵客观实相一样在操纵梦实相。\n\n对旧解释的批评集中在三处：第 83 节说勤勉的知识追求者会发现「连弗洛伊德做梦都想不到的境界」，弗洛伊德只触及外围疆界，荣格也只模糊一瞥；第 244 节引一位研究者的话同意，「自我或意识和潜意识」这套用语本身就严重误导；第 831 节则把达尔文与弗洛伊德的信念合起来看成一个滤网——只有滤得过那滤网的经验才被接受，于是心灵的伟大被忽略了。", "src": "早期课第1册第 23 节；早期课第2册第 57、83 节；早期课第4册第 183、196 节；早期课第6册第 244、259 节；早期课第8册第 341 节；《梦、进化与价值完成》第 889 节；《心灵的本质》第 790、795 节；《个人与群体事件的本质》第 831 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 23 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 57 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 83 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 183 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 196 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 244 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 259 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 341 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 889 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 790 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 795 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 831 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "每个梦都是一种创造的行为", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1443", "zh": "要是没有这一点，就只是一种虚假的创造力而已，没有参与，也没有行动的参与。每个梦都是一种创造的行为，无法以其他方式启动，具有高度的个别性。", "en": "Without this there would be only a sham creativity, without involvement and without the involvement of action. Every dream is a creative act that could be initiated in no other way, highly individual.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第341节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "341", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0030", "zh": "我也不知道所有的答案。不过，就算人类用他那粗笨的方式，也会发现他自己创造了自己的物质宇宙，身体机制的功能和变化比他知道的还多，这却是事实。在睡眠状态下，这些功能也不会静止，反而以一种比他在清醒状态下更直接的形式继续运用。做梦时，他以更真实而且比较不扭曲的方式创造，比起醒时状态，他的物质世界更多是他做梦的自己创造的产物。", "en": "Nor do I know all the answers. It is however a fact that even mankind, in his blundering manner, will discover that he himself creates his own physical universe, and that the mechanisms of the physical body have more functions and varieties than he knows. Nor in the sleeping state are these functions stilled. They continue in an even more direct form than they do when he is awake. He creates when he dreams in a truer and less distorted fashion, and his physical world is much more the product of his dreaming self than it is of his waking state.", "source": "早期课第1册 · 第23节", "year": "1963–64", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第1册", "session": "23", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0121", "zh": "现在再说一次，没有一个心灵行动是静止的。它也不是了无生气。梦世界也许在你们的层面没有物质实相，但在很多方面，它的存在不少于你们以为的实相。不同之处只在于你们聚焦的能量有多少，以及你们把它聚焦在什么方向。", "en": "Now again, no psychic action is static. Nor is it sterile. The dream world may have no material reality in your plane, and yet its existence in many respects is no less than what you consider reality. The difference is only in the amount of energy which you focus and the direction in which you focus it.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第57节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "57", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0795", "zh": "梦经验常常，而且是非常频繁地改变人类的发展方向。同样地，所有这些现象都是透过电磁系统的运作而产生。无意识的自己经常参与的这种梦活动，确实会与你们所谓的灵感或创造性活动相关。", "en": "Dream experiences often, very often, change the course of human events. Again, all this takes place through the operation of electromagnetic systems. There is a definite connection between this constant dream activity undertaken by the unconscious self, and what you call inspiration or creative activity.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第196节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "196", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1165", "zh": "当你成年之后，按照我们的比喻，你就学会成功地操纵梦实相，就像你现在操纵客观实相一样。因为，如果你创造自己的梦，那么你也创造自己的客观环境。 梦实相与物质实相一样真实和实际，但你看，你可以改变它，就像你可以改变物理实相一样。", "en": "When you reach adulthood, following our analogy, then you will learn to be successful in manipulating dream reality as you now manipulate objective reality. For if you create your dreams, you also create your objective environment. The dream reality is as real and actual as physical reality, but it can be changed you see by you, as physical reality can be changed.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第259节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "259", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0690", "zh": "这里也有电子上的意涵，非常重要，电子成分组成了情感经验，很容易在某些颜色氛围里头显示出来。在梦境中，内在经验并不像醒时状态下那样受限，所以可以大大摆脱时间上的障碍，因此有多余的心灵空间留给其他情感经验。", "en": "There are electrical connotations here also that are important, electrical components that compose the emotional experience, and that tend to show themselves in certain auras of color. Within the dreaming state however inner experience is not limited as it is in the waking condition, so that the time barrier can be largely dispensed with. There is an additional psychic space to be filled.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第183节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "183", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "做梦的知性比你们的计算机强", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2680", "zh": "在我讲得太远之前，我在此要强调一点，就是：梦世界不是个漫无目的、非逻辑或不具知性的活动场。只是你们自己的视野关掉了它广大实相的大部分，因为在做梦的知性可胜过你们的计算机。因此，我并不是把思考能力放在不重要的地位——而却是说思考能力以你们所知的样子出现，乃是因为做梦的自己不受阻断的利用了联合的知性和直觉的全部力量。", "en": "(Pause at 10:02.) There is a point here that I want to emphasize before we go too far, and it is this: The dream world is not an aimless, nonlogical, unintellectual field of activity. It is only that your own perspective closes out much of its vast reality, for the dreaming intellect can put your computers to shame. I am not, therefore, putting the intellectual capacities in the background—but I am saying that they emerge as you know them because of the dreaming self’s uninterrupted use of the full power of the united intellect and intuitions.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第889节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "889", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2519", "zh": "因为你们对逻辑的概念非常狭隘，在你看来，好像做梦的自己是不会判断的，或不合“逻辑的”。然而，它却以惊人的辨识力工作，筛检数据，把一些送到身体的某部分，并构成记忆。安眠药也阻碍了梦的判断机能，那是经常被忽略的。事实是，梦涉及了高度的创造行为。这些不仅是直觉地建立的，还是以远超过你对逻辑的概念的逻辑形成的。然后这些创造性行为透过联想过程彼此拼合，极精确地连结在一起，以形成梦的事件。", "en": "Because you have very limited ideas of what logic is, it seems to you that the dreaming self is not critical, or “logical”; yet it works with amazing discrimination, sifting data, sending some to certain portions of the body, and structuring memory. Sleeping pills also impede the critical functions of dreams that are so often overlooked. The facts are that dreams involve high acts of creativity. These are not only intuitively based, but formed with a logic far surpassing your ideas of that quality. These creative acts are then fitted together through associative processes that come together most precisely to form the dream events.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第795节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "795", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2436", "zh": "在大多数梦的硏究里，对梦的游戏性和创造性太过低估。儿童们常透过游戏吓自己，却一直都知道游戏的架构。在花园里的恶鬼在晚餐铃响时就消失了，孩子回到了茶点的安全宇宙。梦常有同样的用处。恐惧被面对了，但黎明到来时，做梦的人醒来吃早餐。那恐惧终被视为无谓的。", "en": "The playfulness and creativity of dreams are vastly underrated in most dream studies. Children often frighten themselves on purpose through games, knowing the game’s framework all the time. The bogeyman in the garden vanishes at the sound of the supper bell. The child returns to the safe universe of tea and biscuits. Dreams often serve the same purpose. Fears are encountered, but the dawn breaks. The dreamer awakes for breakfast. The fears, after all, are seen as groundless. This is not an explanation for all unpleasant dreams by any means, yet it is a reminder that not all such events are neurotic or indicative of future physical problems.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第790节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "790", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "不是弗洛伊德那张清单", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0130", "zh": "在通过这些之后，那勤勉、坚持到底、直觉、有弹性的知识追求者，将会发现连弗洛伊德做梦都想不到的境界。弗洛伊德只触及到外围的疆界，荣格用他那被弗洛伊德引起的骚动而蒙蔽的眼睛，对那更远处的疆域有了模糊的一瞥。", "en": "When these have been passed then the diligent, consistent, intuitive and flexible seeker after knowledge will find horizons of which Freud never dreamed. Freud merely touched the outer boundaries. Jung, with his eyes clouded by the turmoil set up by Freud, glimpsed some further regions, but poorly.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第83节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "83", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1075", "zh": "在他今天所做的工作当中，有一个相当重要的章节。他无意间发现了什么东西，如果他继续努力，就不需要问我梦地点在什么维度了。他离答案很近了。在这个特定的章节，他告诉读者，他怀疑弗洛伊德的用语，自我或意识和潜意识，本身就严重误导你。事实确实如此。", "en": "There is a rather important section in the work he did today. He hit upon something, and if he had continued working, then he would not have needed to ask me in what dimension dream locations exist. He was close to the answer. In this particular section he told the reader that he suspected that Freud’s terms, the ego, or the conscious and the subconscious, had in themselves led you seriously astray. Such is indeed the case.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第244节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "244", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2584", "zh": "现在：达尔文与弗洛伊德的信念，合起来形成了一个不同的滤网。只有当经验滤过了那个滤网才被接受并且被看到。如果基督教把人看作是被原罪所摧折，那么达尔文和弗洛伊德则把人看作是一个有瑕疵族类的一部分，在其中，个人的生命不稳定的存在着，随时听命于族类的需要，而以苟活为主要目标——可是却是一个没有意义的苟活。心灵的伟大被忽略了，个人与大自然的归属感也被侵蚀了，因为仿佛是人类需要牺牲大自然才能苟活。一个人最伟大的梦或是最深的恐惧都变成是腺体分泌不平衡，或是童年创伤所致的精神病的结果。", "en": "Now: The beliefs of [Charles] Darwin and of [Sigmund] Freud3 alike have formed together to give you a different screen. Experience is accepted and perceived only as it is sieved through that screen. If Christendom saw man as blighted by original sin, Darwinian and Freudian views see him as part of a flawed species in which individual life rests precariously, ever at the beck and call of the species’ needs, and with survival as the prime goal — a survival, however, without meaning. The psyche’s grandeur is ignored, the individual’s sense of belonging with nature eroded, for it is at nature’s expense, it seems, that he must survive. One’s greatest dreams and worst fears alike become the result of glandular imbalance, or of neuroses from childhood traumas.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第831节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "831", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B04", "title": "梦里的行动一样真实：身体照单全收", "note": "做梦时你其实什么也没干——这块专门拆这句话。第 173 节先立标准：「对基本的自己来说，梦经验本身就跟任何经验一样真实，这一点再怎么强调也不为过」，所以可以给自己在梦里操作疑难杂症；第 187 节把它写成一句对照表式的判语：对人格来说梦经验跟醒时经验一样真实，「只有自我会去做各种区分」，而那些有电子实相的梦会连同其他经验一起以锁码方式寄存在肉体细胞里，却又独立于细胞。第 126 节说得更细：甚至一个梦的电子实相都会被解码，好让它的效应不只被头脑，也被人体内最微小细胞的最远处体验到；被遗忘已久的梦经验永远被包含在肉体细胞之内。第 254 节举了一个集体例子——对那些受压迫种族没有偏见的人，个人和集体的梦先在梦裡付诸行动，实际造成了变化，然后才发生在历史上。\n\n梦形象也不只是脑中的画面。第 722 节：「你的梦形象存在，它们就与桌椅一样真实，它们是由粒子建造起来的，只有在醒时才看不到」；第 890 节：你们的梦中形体与具体形体一样真实，只是在另一个活动层面，适合它们自己的环境。第 720 节解释那里为什么变化得快：在梦世界里每个感受或概念可以立即被表达及被经验，你的思维就是那个环境里「被制造出的产品」。第 329 节留下一句最短的因果：你梦到的梦形成你的今天。\n\n身体侧的证据也在。第 131 节先总述「梦、思想和心理经验全都有一个电子的实相」；第 193 节说全然主观、只存在于心理时间架构里的经验，会在肉体架构里产生明确的改变，这些改变不是由物质事件造成的；第 240 节给了一个很好玩的细节——梦里你闻到房间里没有的气味，而这份气味记忆被肉体「既忠实又合乎现实地」铭印下来，跟醒时任何真实气味一样；第 189 节则提到做梦时身体的化学比例与所谓正常意识状态下不同。第 43 节把这一块的立场收在一句比较上：你的梦世界实际上比你的醒时世界更接近实相的直接经验，因为醒时你的内在感官被屏蔽了。", "src": "早期课第2册第 43 节；早期课第3册第 126、131 节；早期课第4册第 173、187、189、193 节；早期课第6册第 240、254 节；早期课第7册第 329 节；《未知的实相卷二》第 720、722 节；《梦、进化与价值完成》第 890 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 43 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 126 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 131 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 173 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 187 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 189 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 193 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 240 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 254 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 329 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 720 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 722 节"}, {"n": 13, "id": null, "label": "第 890 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "一样真实，只有自我在做区分", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0598", "zh": "如你们所知，梦可以从许多观点来诠释，因为梦的实相存在于各种不同的现实面向当中。对基本的自己来说，梦经验本身就跟任何经验一样真实，这一点再怎么强调也不为过。因此我们或许可以提供一些操作指南给自己，以便在梦境中解决各种疑难杂症。", "en": "Dreams can be interpreted as you know from many viewpoints, since their reality exists within various aspects of actuality. The dream experience itself, and I cannot stress this too strongly, is as real as any experience to the basic self. It follows that instructions may be given to the self, so that various problems can be solved within the dream situation.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第173节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "173", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0728", "zh": "事实上，具有电子实相的这些梦，连带其他所有的经验，会以锁码的方式一起寄存在肉体细胞里面，却又独立肉体细胞。对人格来说，梦经验就跟醒时经验一样真实，只有自我会去做各种区分。因此，存活下来的人格本身并不会去区分。", "en": "As it is these dreams, having an electrical reality, are deposited in coded form, with all other experience, within but independent of the physical cells. Dream experience is as real to the personality as waking experience. Only the ego makes any distinction. Therefore the personality that has survived makes no distinction on its own.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第187节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "187", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1429", "zh": "你今天可以汲取未来的知识。你明天可以改变过去。你们的时间只在你们的系统之内有效，而且它在你们的系统之内并不是特别有效。你梦到的梦与你所在的教室一样真实。你梦到的梦形成你的今天。", "en": "You can draw upon the knowledge of the future today. You can change the past tomorrow. Your time is valid only within your system, and it is not particularly valid within your system. The dreams that you dream are as real as the classroom in which you sit. The dreams that you dream form your today.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第329节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "329", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0089", "zh": "在某些方面，你知道，你的梦世界，实际上比你的醒时世界，更接近实相的直接经验，在醒时世界，你内在感官的运作被屏蔽起来，以至于脱离了你自己的意识。这并不是说，在你当下的情况中，梦世界对你更重要，而只是说，它包含更多你自己的存在源头有关的真相。", "en": "In some ways you see, your dream world is actually much closer to the direct experience of reality than is your waking world, where the operation of the inner senses is shielded so from your own awareness. This is not to say that the dream world is more important to you in your present situation, merely that it contains more truth about the source of your own existence.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第43节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "43", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "铭进细胞、写进身体", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0437", "zh": "甚至一个梦的电子实相都被解码，好让它的效应不只被头脑，也被人体内最微小细胞的最远处体验到。被遗忘已久的梦经验都会经过电子编码，永远被包含在肉体细胞之内。如果一个效应被人类经验的任何一部分感觉到，那么你们可以确定这样一个效应以其他所有可能的方式被感觉到，不管这样一个效应是不是立刻显而易见。这也是非常重要，应该记住的一点。", "en": "Even the electric reality of a dream is decoded, so that its effects are experienced not only by the brain, but in the furthest reaches of the most minute cells in the human body. Dream experiences long forgotten are forever contained as electrically coded within the cells of the physical body. If an effect is felt in any one portion of human experience, then you can be sure that such an effect is felt in all other possible ways, whether or not such an effect is immediately obvious. This is also an extremely important point to remember.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第126节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "126", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1116", "zh": "你看，在电磁层面上，梦也被铭印在细胞之内。我们举一个简单的例子，那些对受到压迫的种族没有偏见的人。他们个人和集体的梦改变了这种状况。他们在自己的梦中以各种让这种变化能够发生的方式付诸行动。这些梦实际上造成由此产生的变化，然后这个变化才发生在历史上。你看，梦本身的能量和方向有助于改变现状。", "en": "Dreams you see are also imprinted within the cells electromagnetically. We will take a simple example, those who are not prejudiced against those races who are kept downtrodden. They dream individually and collectively of changing the situation. They act out in their dreams the various ways in which such a change about could occur. These dreams actually bring about the resulting change that will then happen in a historic manner. The very energy and direction you see of the dreams themselves will help change the situation.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第254节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "254", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0460", "zh": "我们了解了梦、思想和心理经验全都有一个电子的实相。", "en": "We have seen that dreams and thoughts and psychological experiences all have an electric reality.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第131节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "131", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0757", "zh": "梦一样是由那些全然主观、只存在于心理时间架构中的经验所形成。因此主观的经验会在肉体架构里产生明确的改变，这些改变并不是由物质事件所造成，而是由发生在梦境里的事件所产生，而这个梦境里的事件在你们物质宇宙里并没有实相。", "en": "It is formed equally by those experiences which are purely subjective, and which exist only within the psychological time framework. The subjective experiences therefore result in definite changes within the physical body framework. These changes are not caused because of a physical event, but because of an event occurring within a dream condition which has no reality in your physical universe.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第193节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "193", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1058", "zh": "在梦状态中，你闻到了不在你房间里的气味。气味的记忆是由肉体既忠实又合乎现实地铭印和记录下来，就像醒时状态中任何“真实的”气味一样。", "en": "In the dream state you smell odors that are not in your room. The memory of the odors is imprinted and registered by the physical body as faithfully and realistically as any—quote—“real”—end of quote—odor is in the waking state.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第240节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "240", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0738", "zh": "事实上，神经的形式在这里非常重要，新陈代谢机制也是如此。然而，了解做梦的人如何建构其梦影像的方法，也可以让你们更清楚了解其他方面的建构。化学的比例在这里也很重要；也就是说，做梦时的化学比例，不同于你们所谓的正常意识状态下身体呈现的化学比例。", "en": "Nerve patterns are indeed very important here, and mechanisms of metabolism. An understanding of the methods used by the dreamer to construct dream images will, however, allow you to understand more clearly those other constructions. The chemical percentages are important here; that is, the chemical percentages vary from those found in the body during what you call normal states of consciousness.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第189节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "189", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "梦形象有自己的粒子", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2201", "zh": "梦的世界与你自己的世界一样的有组织，但你在醒时状态并不聚焦在那内在的组织上。你的梦形象存在，它们就与桌椅一样真实，它们是由粒子建造起来的，只有在醒时才看不到。", "en": "The dream world is as organized as your own, but from the waking state you do not focus upon that inner organization. Your dream images exist. They are quite as real as a table or a chair. They are built up from particles, invisible only from the waking situation.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第722节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "722", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2682", "zh": "换言之，形体存在于你们所认知的那些层面之外的许多其它层面。你们的梦中形体与你们的具体形体一样真实，只不过是在另一个活动层面。它们适合它们自己的环境，而相当令人想起在（你们的）世界开始时的那种形体。", "en": "Form exists at many other levels than those you recognize, in other words. Your dream forms are quite as real as your physical ones. They simply fit into their own environment at another level of activity, and they are quite reminiscent of the kinds of forms that you had in the beginning of [your] world.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第890节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "890", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2168", "zh": "可是，在梦世界里，每个感受或概念可以立即被表达及被经验。物质世界里有你们建造的建筑物——那是说，它们并不会从土地本身自然的跳出来。以同样的方式，你的思维就成了梦世界里“被制造出的产品”。它们是那环境的一部分，出现在其实相里，虽然它们经常改变形状与模样，而实质的制造出来的物品则否。", "en": "In the dream world, however, each feeling or idea can be immediately expressed and experienced. The physical world has buildings in it that you manufacture — that is, they do not spring up naturally from the ground itself. In the same way, your thoughts are “manufactured products” in the dream world. They are a part of the environment and appear within its reality, though they change shape and form constantly, as physically manufactured objects do not.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第720节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "720", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B05", "title": "梦是排演场：你在里面试演可能的自己与可能的解法", "note": "这块讲梦最实用的一面：它替你试做。第 144 节先把机制说清楚——任何人都可以在梦中建构很多可能性，遇到物质世界的问题时，他会在梦里尝试各种不同的解决方法；这些可能性「于是在梦场域之内变成现实」，如果他在物质场域里以同样方式行动，它们一定存在——不是虚构、不是想象、也不会消失。第 144 节又补：他梦到这个梦、选择这个可能性作为解决之道时，就已经在潜意识里选择了他会在物质场域建构的事件。第 187 节称此为梦戏剧使人格可以演出各种不同的可能性，然后最有效的戏剧才在物质实相中反应出来；第 707 节说你私人的心灵密切关怀你俗世的存在，在梦境里你与可能的行动打交道，常常找出解决之道；第 721 节把这件事说得很大——在梦里每个人都在解决他自己的问题或挑战，连病人可以梦到自己治愈自己的病。第 640 节给了一个说法：梦境供给你一个「尝试的架构」。\n\n为什么你在梦里做的选择重要？因为梦的自己与「可能性的自己」是同一条线。第 233 节说梦的自己很大程度意识到那个我们称之为可能性的自己，有点像它的双胞胎；第 232 节用一个苹果解释你们的看不见——你可能梦见握着一个苹果，醒来苹果不见了，这不代表苹果不存在，只代表你在醒时状态没有觉知到它的实相；同一个做梦的我有着它由过去之梦组成的记忆，而经验着可能性事件的另一个我有着同样的身份与记忆，两者之间持续交换潜意识讯息（第 232 节、第 233 节）。第 234 节提醒这不是封闭系统：许多梦的方面会在物质宇宙里变得真实——你梦过一个不存在于物质实相中的孩子吗？那些通常存在于可能性的领域。第 231 节则说潜意识多少熟悉可能性，会以问题解决的方式检视它们，帮助自我决定哪一个可能性事件该被选为自己的经验。\n\n挑选本身就是梦的关键属性。第 92 节说，看似毫不相干的符号大杂烩里，梦最重要的特征其实是甄别——做梦者从看似无穷无尽的可能性中精挑细选，只拣对自己有意义的象征与物体。第 115 节加了一句：很多观念、巨大的进步和实际的发明，只不过暂时搁置在梦世界里，等到某个人接受它们是自己实相架构之内的可能性为止。第 785 节把它写成一个流程：许多信息甚至没到达脑子，但心灵对它们有知觉；在梦境，这些数据被转成「可能发生的事件的反映，可能的顺序的试映」。第 309 节则提醒：所有行动都会被表达出来，你现在某种程度具有一个根源本质，会有很多类似的梦——若你遇见可能的自己，你立刻会知道那是你自己的未知部分。", "src": "早期课第3册第 92、115、144 节；早期课第4册第 187 节；早期课第5册第 231、232、233、234 节；早期课第7册第 309 节；《个人实相的本质》第 640 节；《未知的实相卷二》第 707、721 节；《心灵的本质》第 785 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 92 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 115 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 144 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 187 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 231 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 232 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 233 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 234 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 309 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 640 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 707 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 721 节"}, {"n": 13, "id": null, "label": "第 785 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "问题先在梦里试做", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0496", "zh": "牵涉的东西太多了。这个人、任何人，都可以在梦中建构很多可能性。在物质世界遇到问题，他可能会是企图在梦中处理它们，尝试各种不同的解决方法，藉此解决它们。", "en": "There is much involved. The individual, any individual, may construct many possibilities in the dream world. Having problems in the physical world, he may attempt to solve them through working them out on a dream basis, trying various solutions.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第144节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "144", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0497", "zh": "这些可能性于是在梦场域之内变成现实。如果他在物质场域之内以同样的方式行动，那它们一定存在。它们不是虚构，它们也不是想像，它们不会消失。它们就是存在另一个现实场域之内的实相。", "en": "These possibilities then become actualities within the dream field. They exist as surely as they would if he acted in the same manner within the physical field. They are not myths, they are not imaginary, they do not vanish. They exist as reality within another field of actuality.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第144节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "144", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0499", "zh": "当他梦到这个梦，选择这个可能性作为他的问题解决之道时，他就已经在潜意识选择他会在物质场域之内建构的事件了。", "en": "When he dreamed the dream, and chose this possibility as the solution to his problems, he had already subconsciously chosen which event he would construct within the physical field.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第144节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "144", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0722", "zh": "梦让自我有能力解决迫在眉睫的问题，梦戏剧使人格可以演出各种不同的可能性，这些可能性都能当成实相来体验。然后最有效的戏剧才会在物质实相中反应出来。", "en": "One dream will enable the ego to solve pressing immediate problems. The dream drama will enable the personality to act out various possibilities which are experienced as reality. The most effective drama is then reacted to in physical reality.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第187节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "187", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2183", "zh": "甚至那些从未见过飞机的人也可以在一眨眼之间从一个地方旅行到另一个地方，而穷人被喂饱，无知的人变聪明，而病人健康了。那可能在实质上被阻碍的创造力被表达了。的确，当那饥饿的人醒来时仍是饥饿的，生病的人醒来时并不比他们以前更健康。可是，以更深的说法，在梦境里每个人会在解决他自己的问题或挑战。做梦时一个人努力解决引起他病的问题，能治愈他自己的病。做梦时，那饥饿的人能发现找到食物的方法或获得买食物的钱的方法。做梦是个实际的活动，如果这点被了解了的话，以你们的说法，它就会甚至更实际。", "en": "Even those who have never seen an airplane can travel from place to place in the twinkling of an eye, and the poor are fed, the ignorant are wise, the sick are well. The creativity that may be physically hampered is expressed. It is true that the hungry man, awakening, is still hungry. The ill may awaken no healthier than they were before. In deeper terms, however, in the dream state each person will be working out his or her own problems or challenges. Dreaming, a person can cure himself or herself of a disease, working through the problems that caused it. Dreaming, the hungry individual can discover ways to find food, or to procure the money to buy it. Dreaming is a practical activity. If it were understood as such, it would be even more practical in your terms.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第721节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "721", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1898", "zh": "梦境供给你一个尝试的架构，在其中探寻可能的行动，而决定要把哪些付诸实行。不止是上一节提到的梦魇，其他的梦也遵循着一种治疗节奏，那比任何药物都有效。安眠药常常会干扰梦的治疗作用。", "en": "The dream state provides you with a trial framework in which you explore probable actions and decide upon the ones you want to physically materialize. Not only nightmares, as mentioned earlier (in the last session), but many other dreams follow rhythms of a therapeutic nature far more effectively than any that are drug-induced. Sleeping pills can interfere with this activity.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第640节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "640", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "梦的自己与可能的自己", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2111", "zh": "现在：你私人的心灵密切的关怀你俗世的存在，而在你的梦境里，你与可能的行动打交道，而常常找出在那个情况里升出的有关一连串可能事件之间的解决之道。", "en": "Now. Your private psyche is intimately concerned with your earthly existence, and in your dream state you deal with probable actions, and often work out in that condition the solutions to problems or questions that arise having to do with probable sequences of events.4 On many occasions then you set yourself a problem — “Shall I do this or that?” — and form a dream in which you follow through the probable futures that would “result” from the courses available. While you are sleeping and dreaming, your chemical and hormonal activity faithfully follows the courses of the dreams. Even in your accepted reality, then, to that extent in such a dream you react to probable events as well as to the events chosen for waking physical experience. Your daily life is affected, because in such a dream you deal with probable predictabilities. You are hardly alone, however, so each individual alive also has his and her private dreams, and these help form the accepted probability sequence of the following day, and of “time to come.” The personal decisions all add up to the global happenings on any given day.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第707节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "707", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1027", "zh": "你们没有觉知到你们整体的经验，因为你们是以一种连续性的方式来经验事件。所以你们只能以一种连续性的方式来了解你们的梦。你们很不熟悉你们梦的自己所有梦的经验，也几乎不了解它们的含义。梦的自己到一个很大的程度，是意识到那个我们称之为可能性的自己。这个可能性的自己有点像这个梦的人格的双胞胎自己，因为不管是这个梦的自己的经验，或是可能性自己的经验，都没有发生在物质实相的整体范围里。", "en": "You are not aware of your experiences in their entirety, for you experience events in a consecutive manner. You are therefore aware of your dreams only in a consecutive manner. You are hardly familiar with all of the dream experiences of your dreaming self, and barely familiar with any of their implications. The dreaming self is to some considerable degree conscious of the self which we shall here term the probable self. The probable self is somewhat like a twin self to the dreaming personality, for neither the experiences of the dreaming self nor the probable self occur within the complete radius of physical reality.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第233节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "233", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1017", "zh": "可能性以同样的方式存在。它们对你来说不是具体的，就像梦对你来说也不是具体的。例如，你可能梦见握着一个苹果，然后醒来苹果就不见了。这并不代表这个苹果不存在。它只是代表了在醒时的状态，你没有觉知到它的实相。你没有经验它，或感知它。以同样的方式，你没有物质上的感知或经验到可能事件的真实。", "en": "Now, probabilities exist in the same manner. They simply are not concrete for you, as dreams are not concrete for you. You may dream for example of holding an apple, then wake up and the apple is gone. This does not mean that the apple did not exist. It simply means that in the waking state you are not aware of its reality. You do not experience it, or perceive it. In the same manner you do not perceive or experience physically the actuality of probable events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第232节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "232", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1018", "zh": "可能性的系统并不如那些存在于梦境中的事件那么容易被感知到，因为当你在梦的状态，这是你比较熟悉的。通常自我更觉知到梦的事件。现在那个自己，你们梦里的那个我，可以十分合理的相比于那个经验着可能性事件的自己。", "en": "The system of probabilities is not as easily perceived even as those events that exist within the dream state, for with these you are familiar, while you are in the dream state. Often the ego is even made aware of dream events. Now the self that is the I of your dreams can quite legitimately be compared to the self that experiences probable events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第232节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "232", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1021", "zh": "现在通过催眠它可以被探知，这个做梦的我有着它过去存在的记忆，是由过去的梦所组成的。如果你喜欢的话，你可以称这个是梦中人格，但它等同于另一个自己。现在，没有选择在物质宇宙里经验的，这个经验着可能性事件的我，有着和这个梦的自己同样的身份和记忆。在自己的这些部分之间，有着持续的潜意识讯息的交换。", "en": "Now through hypnosis it has been ascertained that this dreaming I has memory of its past existence, which is made up of past dreams. You may call this the dreaming personality if you prefer, but it amounts to another self. Now the I who experiences probable events, not chosen for experience in the physical universe, has the same kind of identity and memory as this dream self. There is a constant subconscious interchange of information between these portions of the self.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第232节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "232", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1042", "zh": "就任何方面来说，它都远不是一个封闭的系统。它不只喂养了物质宇宙，而且在其中，你们自己的梦的很多方面都变得真实。你梦过一个苹果吗？你梦过一个不存在于物质实相中的孩子吗？通常这些存在于可能性的领域。", "en": "It is far from a closed system in any way. Not only does it feed the physical universe, but in it many aspects of your own dreams become actual. Do you dream of an apple? Do you dream of a child, who has no existence in physical reality? Often these exist in the field of probability.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第234节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "234", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1012", "zh": "当这情形发生的时候，它通常是从潜意识里，从梦境渗漏出来，因为潜意识多少是熟悉可能性的，而到某种程度以问题解决的方式来经验这些可能性。它在心中以自我的目的来检视这些可能性，因此帮助自我来决定哪一个可能性事件应该被选为它自己的经验。", "en": "When this does happen it usually occurs as a bleed-through from the subconscious from the dream state, for the subconscious is somewhat acquainted with probabilities, and to some degree experiences these in a problem-solving manner. It views various probabilities with the purposes of the ego in mind, and therefore aids the ego in its decisions as to which probable events it should choose for its own experience.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第231节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "231", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "甄别：从可能性里挑出那一个", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0181", "zh": "因为从看似无穷无尽的可能性之中，我们个体的做梦者都非常小心地加以区别，只选择那些对他有意义的象征或物体；而且那些梦物体最符合他的目的。甚至似乎和琐碎的日常事件有关的一个简单的梦，实际上和更多事物有关联。 [看起来梦境是毫不相干的符号、事件的大杂烩，实际上梦境的最重要的特征就是甄别。从看似无限的可能性中， 做梦的人其实精挑细选，只拣出对自己有意义的梦境物体或符号，以及最切合自己需要的梦境物体。你认真审视就会发现，这些梦境物体不仅包含与梦者日常意识层面有关的信息，还与许多其他层面相关联，比如前世与来世。 ]", "en": "For out of a seemingly endless number of possibilities, our individual dreamer actually discriminates with great care, choosing only those dream objects or symbols that have meaning to him; and those dream objects that can best serve his purposes. And even a simple dream that would seem to be concerned with trivial daily events is in reality concerned with much more.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第92节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "92", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0404", "zh": "很多观念、巨大的进步和实际的发明，只不过暂时搁置在梦世界里，等到某个人接受它们是他实相架构之内的可能性为止。如果不是我知道自我的本质，我会想不通为什么人类这么常怀疑梦实相。", "en": "Many concepts, huge advancements and practical inventions, simply wait in abeyance in the world of dreams until some man accepts them as possibilities within his frame of reality. Why man so often mistrusts the dream reality would escape me, if I did not know the nature of the ego.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第115节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "115", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2366", "zh": "许多信息甚至没到达脑子（不过，心智对这些数据是有知觉的）。在人类之中，对于在任何方面将影响到这个有机体的有关讯息，心灵——身体的结构在每一刻都有一个完整的、最新的画面。所有行动，都是在这些讯息已知的情况下采取的。再次的，在梦境这种数据被转成假实质的画面——可能发生的事件的反映，可能的顺序的试映。这些讯息，在暂时聚焦于「内在实相剧场」的意识之前闪现。", "en": "Much information does not even reach the brain (the mind is aware of such data, however). In man, the psychic-physical structure has at every moment a complete up-to-date picture of pertinent information about all events that will in any way affect the organism. All actions are taken with this information available. In the dream state such data becomes transformed, again, into pseudophysical pictures — reflections of events that might occur, previews of probable sequences. These are flashed before a consciousness that momentarily focuses upon the inner rather than the outer arena of reality.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第785节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "785", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1374", "zh": "所有的行动都会被表达出来。此外，现在你某种程度上具有一个根源本质，你会有很多梦是类似的。你根本还谈不上认同这些可能的自己，但是，如果你遇见它们，你立刻就会知道你已经找到你自己的未知部分。", "en": "All action will be expressed. Now to some degree you have a root nature, then, and many of your dreams will be similar. You are far from identical with these probable selves, and yet if you met them you would instantly know that you had found unknown portions of yourself.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第309节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "309", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B06", "title": "带回来的是一张转译过的照片", "note": "很多初学者卡在我记不住梦所以梦没用这一句。赛斯的回答分两层：带回的是转译过的，而不是原物。第 570 节给了最好的意象——「大半的梦是像你从一个你大部分已遗忘了的旅程带回来的有生命的风景明信片」，你的意识已经重新定位到物质实相，那个梦是「把更深的经历转译为可认识的形式的企图」，而梦中形象极度密码式，是潜藏在梦底下那个事件的信号，那事件基本上无法破解。同节又说，梦是多次元的艺术产品，生者与死者都能参与，所以灵感与启示才常常是梦的一部分。第 569 节说明转移不是失控：意识由外在实相转到内在实相时有特定的步骤、确切的改变，而这些改变不是无目的的，走的是可预期的路；世代以来「说法者」一直在教作梦的人在其他环境里该怎么做、怎么把对他们现在人格有利的东西带回来。\n\n第二层：漏掉不等于损失。第 174 节坦白说，就一般记忆而言你们只记得一部分的梦，而且经常来自比较表层的；第 789 节解释为什么这样就够——在梦前状态你参与了一种更能感知的心理组织，梦醒时你只把能被具体认知与利用的数据带回肉身，而你的经验与知识也在确定性的其他层面生长。第 795 节则提醒一个陷阱：你对梦的信念会渲染你的记忆与诠释，在醒转那一点你们常做「最后一分钟的调整」，让梦与有意识的期望更一致。第 88 节给了原因的另一半：潜意识有层次，每一层都有自己的活动，会转译、象征化，也往往会扭曲有效的资料，视资料穿过的是哪一层而定。\n　\n好消息是没被记住也照样在工作。第 93 节说得很多：一个梦虽源于某一层，但在每一层都有意义，只是意义可能不一样，它自动被潜意识各层转译；自我这个「前线的代表人物」会在不知情、甚至还改变路线的情形下回应他并没有觉察的梦；如果来自内我的讯息够强，就会跨越意识障碍，而这类讯息通常先以梦的形式出现，等情况有需要时才释放到意识心智；即使意识上没有认知，透过梦接收的讯息也自动被当成行动的依据或被消化吸收。第 87 节补一句：没有必要给你肤浅的分析，因为梦通常透露人格几个层次可以利用的知识——潜意识感知并带入脉络的讯息，自我可能根本无法理解，但整个人格还是会用它。第 671 节留了一句安全的话：你会从梦中接受到大半与你醒时有意识的信念相符的资料，你完全不会被梦摆布。\n\n最后给一句定位：第 909 节说，想象、创造、心灵感应、千里眼、做梦的能力，以及逻辑与推理的机能，这些你直接知道——「你知道你做梦」，那是直接的经验，比任何仪器给你的二手证据更近。", "src": "早期课第3册第 87、88、93 节；早期课第4册第 174 节；《灵魂永生》第 569、570 节；《个人实相的本质》第 671 节；《心灵的本质》第 789、795 节；《梦、进化与价值完成》第 909 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 87 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 88 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 93 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 174 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 569 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 570 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 671 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 789 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 795 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 909 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "带回的是风景明信片", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1777", "zh": "所有这些并不如它看起来那么玄秘。几乎每个人都有意识上的古怪经验，而直觉地知道他们更大的经验是不受限于物质实相的范围内的。大半的梦是像你从一个你大部分已遗忘了的旅程带回来的有生命的风景明信片。你的意识已经再次地定位于物质实相了，而那个梦是个把更深的经历转译为可认识的形式的企图。梦中形象也是极度“密码式”的，是潜藏在梦底下的事件的信号，这事件基本上是无法破解的。", "en": "(Pause at 9:23.) All of this is not as esoteric as it might seem. Almost every individual has had bizarre experiences with consciousness, and knows intuitively that their greater experience is not limited to physical reality. Most dreams are like animated postcards brought back from a journey that you have returned from and largely forgotten. Your consciousness is already oriented again to physical reality; the dream, an attempt to translate the deeper experience into recognizable forms. The images within the dream are also highly coded, and are signals for underlying events that are basically not decipherable.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第570节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "570", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1775", "zh": "在我们下一章里，我再详细地谈这些。至于目前，你们只要觉悟到，在意识由外在实相转移到内在实相时，会发生特定的步骤、确切的改变，而这些改变不是无目的的；并且知道，意识是由一条非常可预期的路离开而到达它的许多目的地，就已经够了。世代以来，“说法者”一直在教作梦的人在这些其他的环境该怎么做，也一直在教他们如何带回能对他们现在的人格有利的情报。一个人按照他的意图、当前目标和发展的不同，对这些旅行可以有不同程度的觉察。例如，有些人回想得非常清楚，却常常因为有意识的概念而误解了他们的经验。", "en": "In our next chapter I will speak of these in some detail. For now it is sufficient to realize that specific steps, definite alterations, occur as consciousness is shifted from the exterior to the interior reality, and that these changes are not random; that consciousness leaves through a very predictable route to its many destinations. Through the ages the Speakers have taught dreamers how to manipulate in these other environments. They have taught them how to bring back information that could be used for the good of the present personality. According to the intent, present purpose, and development, an individual may be aware of these travels to varying degrees. Some have excellent recall, for example, but often misinterpret their experience because of conscious ideas.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第569节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "569", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2410", "zh": "也许借由更能感知的心理组织才最能表现其特征。在梦前状态，你参与了这种组织，虽然在梦醒时，你只将能被具体认知与利用的数据带回给你的肉身。非常重要的是，记住你的经验与知识也在确实性的其它层面生长。即使在你实质的一生里，你的经验也并不被局限于只是传统的实质事件。那些寻常事件是由发生在这些其它层面的创造性冲动中升起的。", "en": "This is characterized perhaps most of all by more perceptive psychological organizations. In the predream state you participate in such organizations, although you bring back home to your physical self — in the form of dreams — only data that can be recognized and used in physical terms. It is highly important to remember that your experience and knowledge grow at those other levels of actuality. Even during your physical lifetime your experience is not confined to conventional physical events alone. Those usual events arise from the creative impetus that occurs at these other levels.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第789节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "789", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0617", "zh": "就一般的记忆来讲，你们显然只记得一部分的梦，而且这些梦经常是来自比较表层。", "en": "In normal memory you recall, obviously, but a slight percentage of your dreams, and usually these are from more surface levels.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第174节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "174", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2505", "zh": "你对梦的信念会渲染你的记忆，以及你对它们的诠释，因此在醒转的那一点，怀着卓越的心理上的欺骗，你们常做最后一分钟的调整，以使你的梦与你有意识的期望更加一致（Your beliefs about dreams color your memory and interpretation of them, so that at the point of waking, with magnificent psychological duplicity, you often make last-minute adjustments that bring your dreams more in line with your conscious expectations.）。例如，常附属于梦的意象的性象征是过分简化的，它们使你以一个特定方式诠释你的梦（The sexual symbols usually attached to dream images are highly simplistic, for example. They program you to interpret your dreams in a given manner.）。 等我们一会儿……", "en": "Your beliefs about dreams color your memory and interpretation of them, so that at the point of waking, with magnificent psychological duplicity, you often make last-minute adjustments that bring your dreams more in line with your conscious expectations. The sexual symbols usually attached to dream images are highly simplistic, for example. They program you to interpret your dreams in a given manner.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第795节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "795", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0167", "zh": "不是所有的梦都是千里眼梦。由于无意识或潜意识是有层次的，所有这些层次的每一个同样都有它们自己的活动，而且会转译、象征化，往往也会扭曲有效的资料，根据的是不同程度的潜意识，资料就是透过它才能够浮现。", "en": "All dreams are not clairvoyant. As there are levels of unconsciousness or sub-consciousness, so do each of these levels have their own activities and translate, symbolize and often distort valid data, according to the different degree of sub-consciousness through which the data were able to emerge.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第88节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "88", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "各层各自转译，各层各自用", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0209", "zh": "那么，梦来自你们所谓潜意识的各层。但是一般来说，任何一个梦虽然源于某一层，但在每一层都有意义。只不过意义可能不一样。也就是说，这一个梦可能是说出不同事情或捎来不同讯息的一个方法，这一个梦自动被潜意识各层转译，成为潜意识的任何一层给梦象征的诠释。", "en": "Dreams, then, come from various levels of what you call the subconscious. But as a rule any particular dream, although it originates in a particular level, will nevertheless have meaning on all levels. The meaning however may well not be the same. That is, the particular dream may be a method of saying different things or bringing different messages, the one particular dream automatically being translated by the various levels of the subconscious in terms of the interpretation given by any particular subconscious level to the dream symbolism.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0210", "zh": "这个梦可以说是捎给自己众多层次的一个讯息。打个比喻，想像一下每一层潜意识都人格化，成为一个人格，然后想像它受制于一个或更多梦的解释，它看着梦影像在上面闪烁的一个银幕。", "en": "The dream could be said to be a message to the multitudinous levels of the self. For purposes of analogy only, imagine that each subconscious layer is personified into a personality, who is then subjected to rendition of a dream or more, who watches a screen upon which the dream images flicker.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0222", "zh": "现在。有意识的自己在不知情，往往还会改变路线和方向的状况下，回应这些他通常没有觉察的梦。自我、有意识的自我、所谓有意识的自己，只是前线的代表人物而已，受到他自己众多的区域或部分支持，他对它们一无所知，它们的讯息只能透过梦的通讯传送给他。", "en": "Now. The conscious self responds without knowing it, often changing course and direction, to these dreams of which he is often not aware. The ego, the conscious ego, the so-called conscious self, is only the front man in the front lines, supported by multitudinous areas or portions of himself that he does not know, and whose messages come to him only through the correspondence of dreams.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0226", "zh": "如果来自内我的讯息够强，就会跨越意识障碍，有意识的自我接收到它们简直乐透了。但这样的直觉通常先以梦的形式出现，稍后才显现于自我面前；而且很多这样的讯息提前出现在梦中，在情况有需要时才释放到意识心智。", "en": "Messages from the inner self if strong enough will pass through the conscious barriers, and the conscious ego is only too happy to receive them. But such intuitions have usually first appeared in dream form, appearing to the ego later; and many such messages appear in dreams ahead of time, to be released to the conscious mind when situations demand it.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0235", "zh": "然而，我说过意识心智只是全我的一小部分，这样透过梦接收到的讯息，自动被当成行动的依据，或者是被消化吸收，不管意识上有没有认知。", "en": "However, I have said that the conscious mind is but a small portion of the whole self, and the information thus received through dreams is automatically acted upon or assimilated, regardless of conscious cognition.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0161", "zh": "没有必要给你一个肤浅的分析，因为梦通常透露人格的几个层次可以利用的知识；在意识上，被潜意识感知到并且带入有意义的脉络中的讯息，对自我来说可能根本无法理解，虽然如此，整个人格还是会利用它。 [梦境传递的信息，通常可以应用在人格的几个层面上。 尽管经由潜意识察觉并诠释含义的信息，不能被外在自我有意识地完全理解，但是这些信息仍然会被整体人格所利用。]", "en": "There is no point in giving you a superficial analysis, since dreams usually impart knowledge that can be utilized by several levels of the personality; though consciously the information perceived and brought into meaningful context by the subconscious may not be at all comprehensible to the ego, it is nevertheless utilized by the whole personality.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第87节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "87", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1995", "zh": "你会由梦中接受到那些大半与你醒时有意识的信念相符合的资料。如先前提及的，梦中和醒时之间直相互作用，可以说，在其内，新的信念可以被拿来尝试。就那方面而言，你完全不会为你的梦所摆布。", "en": "You will accept from your dreams that information that largely agrees with your waking conscious beliefs. There is interaction, as mentioned previously, in which new beliefs are tried out, so to speak. In that regard, you are not at the mercy of your dreams in any meaning of the word.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第671节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "671", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "做梦本身就是直接经验", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2767", "zh": "那些“属性”就是想象、创造、心灵感应、千里眼和做梦的能力，以及逻辑与推理的机能。你知道你做梦，你知道你思考，那些是直接的经验。（停顿。）任何时候你用仪器探索进实相的本质时，不论那仪器多么精密，你都是在看一种二手的证据。举例来说，做梦的主观证据，比一个关于扩张中的宇宙、黑洞、甚至原子及分子本身的证据，要远较“令人信服”且不可推翻。虽然在许多方面，仪器的确可以是非常有利的，但它们给你的却仍只是二手而非一手的探查工具——而它们远比思想、感受与直觉的主观属性，更扭曲了实相的本质。", "en": "Those “properties” are the faculties of the imagination, creativity, telepathy, clairvoyance, and dreaming, as well as the functions of logic and reason. You know that you dream. You know that you think. Those are direct experiences. (Pause.) Anytime you use instruments to probe into the nature of reality, you are looking at a kind of secondary evidence, no matter how excellent the instruments may be. The subjective evidence of dreaming, for example, is far more “convincing” and irrefutable than is the evidence for an expanding universe, black holes, or even atoms and molecules themselves. Although instruments can indeed be most advantageous in many ways, they still present you with secondary rather than primary tools of investigation—and they distort the nature of reality far more than the subjective attributes of thoughts, feelings, and intuitions do.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第909节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "909", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B07", "title": "梦没有通用词典：象征是内我为你私做的", "note": "这一页要先把「查梦」这条路堵住。第 641 节的说法很干脆：在梦里一件东西可能是象征符号，可是「没有一个放诸四海皆准的梦的象征符号」，因为个人经验中有太多的变化；有时你确实在梦里触及存在最深的源头，但那儿表达还是很个人化，不能把某个「无意识的」固定意义加在所有相同象征上。第 568 节从别的实相系统看同一件事：上升或下降、光或暗的象征，对那些具有不同感知机构的系统根本无意义；你们的宗教象征是内我为了配合你们在物质宇宙里认为合理的基本假设而技巧地选出来的，梦中传来的其他情报也会用类似象征，但那象征只是为内我所用。\n\n象征也确实有文化与族群的分量，但只是「大概」。第 92 节说很多梦象征大致意味着同一件事，但只对某一族群在物质层面一段相对短时间内体验实相的人如此——火对还没学会用火取暖煮食的人，就不可能是它现在的梦义；那古老的火的象征仍然存在，作为神秘的辉煌或毁灭，后来才变成炉边温暖的象征，所以梦象征某种程度上有文化内涵（第 92 节）。同节还提醒：每个梦世界都是种种个人式的象征混合物，别人看你的梦会觉得毫无可比性。\n\n那么解读时该看什么？看内我怎么挑的。第 93 节说，所谓细腻区别指的是「内我在选择它个人化的象征上所做的惊人工作」，同一个梦里的统一象征会被潜意识各层各自解读——它举了一个「tub」（旧船/浴缸）的例子，同一个词在最表层是「有一天我会是一艘旧破船」，第二层被读成一台漏水的洗衣机，再导向下一个象征，属于一个前世里漏水的旧船。第 93 节又用看电影作比：许多人看同一部电影，每人对剧情象征有不同诠释，潜意识每一层对同一梦元素也有不同诠释；那个梦也可以是像飞机上的人用无线电把一出编码象征的梦戏剧直接送进下方人的心智机制，由他们自动转译。第 94 节给的操作要求很短：在解读你的梦时，我们必须改变我们的焦点，因为透过有意识检视而看似合理的解读经常被扭曲；梦源自不同层面，所以要按它们所属实相界域本来就有的特定象征来解读（第 92 节）。\n\n第 787 节干脆列出人们真正想问的问题——梦的事件有多真实？梦有何意义？梦如何影响日常生活？并指出每个人都觉知梦那种可惊的亲密本质；第 109 节则说这类内在象征操纵和你们的呼吸或走路一样自动（早期课第3册第 109 节），而观念在梦中实现、接着在物质上被建构出来时，也会回过头来丰富梦宇宙、增加那里在象征上可能有的自由（第 109 节）。", "src": "早期课第3册第 92、93、94 节；《灵魂永生》第 568 节；《个人实相的本质》第 641 节；《心灵的本质》第 787 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 92 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 93 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 94 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 568 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 641 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 787 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "没有人人通用的梦词典", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1907", "zh": "在梦里面，一件东西可能是个象征符号，可是没有一个放诸四海皆准的梦的象征符号，在个人经验中有太多的变化。的确有时候在梦里，你会触及存在的一些最深源头，但甚至在那儿，那个存在的表达是很个人化的，而不能以一个“无意识的”意义加诸在所有相同的象征符号上。", "en": "In them of course one object may be a symbol, but there is no such thing as an overall statement of dream symbolism, in which a given symbol will have a general meaning. There are too many variations in personal experience. It is true that in dreams you do reach some of the deepest sources of your being at times, but even there, the expression of that being is far too individualistic to assign the same kind of “unconscious” meaning to overall symbols.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第641节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "641", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1767", "zh": "上升或下降，光或暗的象征对那些具有不同的感知机构的其他实相系统来说将是无意义的。虽然你们的宗教是围绕着一个颠扑不破的“真理种子”建造出来的，它所用的象征却是由“内我”很技巧地选择出来，以配合这物质宇宙里那些你们认为合理的基本假设。其他的情报，例如在梦中的，一般来说也会以相同的象征给你们。不过，这象征本身只是为“内我”所用，它并非天生就属于内在实相的。", "en": "Now: The symbolism of ascent or descent, or of light and dark, would be meaningless to other realities with different perceptive mechanisms. While your religions are built around an enduring kernel of truth, the symbolism used was craftily selected by the inner self in line with its knowledge of those root assumptions you hold as valid in the physical universe. Other information, in dreams for example, will also be given to you with the same symbolism, generally speaking. The symbolism itself, however, was simply used by the inner self. It does not inherently belong to inner reality.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第568节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "568", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2384", "zh": "当人们声称对梦的本质感兴趣时，他们通常心中有些特定的问题，就如：「梦的事件有多真实？」「梦有何意义？」「梦如何影响日常生活？」每个人都觉知梦之可惊的亲密本质（Each person is aware of the astonishingly intimate nature of dreams.）。虽然如此，在你们的经验中，某些象征符号似乎有相当的普遍性。", "en": "When people profess an interest in the nature of dreams, they usually have certain set questions in mind, such as: “How real are dream events?” “What do dreams mean?” “How do they affect daily life?” Each person is aware of the astonishingly intimate nature of dreams. Despite this, certain symbols seem to be fairly universal in your experience.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第787节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "787", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "象征有文化层，但只是大概", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0198", "zh": "你知道有很多，但当然不是全部，梦象征大概意味着同一件事，但只是大概，而且只对某一族群在物质层面的一段相对来说算短的时间内体验实相的人类而言是这样。比方说火的象征，就一个梦象征而言，对出生于人类学会生火取暖或煮食之前的人来说，并不是以它现在存在的方式存在。", "en": "You know that many, but not all by any means, dream symbols approximately mean the same thing, but only approximately, and only for a particular group of human beings who experienced reality on the physical plane for a comparatively short period. The symbol of fire for example as a dream symbol, simply did not exist in the way that it does now to men born before mankind learned to use fire for warmth, or to cook his food.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第92节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "92", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0199", "zh": "火的古老象征现在依然存在，火是神秘的辉煌或毁灭。只不过后来它的确变成炉边温暖的一个象征，所以某种程度上梦象征具有文化内涵。", "en": "The old symbol of fire still does exist, fire as mysterious grandeur or destruction. Only later did it become a symbol of hearth and warmth, so that to some extent dream symbols are cultural.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第92节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "92", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0196", "zh": "他们于是会认为两者之间没有任何真正的比较可言，因为每个个别的梦世界都是种种不同个人主义式象征的混合物，即使它们被投射到一种意识心智一无所知的宇宙中也一样。", "en": "They will then think that there is no real comparison to be made between the two, since each individual dream world would be a conglomeration of diverse individualistic symbols, even if they were projected into a type of universe of which the conscious mind was ignorant.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第92节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "92", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0201", "zh": "所以他们的梦世界在某些方面没有你们以为的那么不同。某些象征在梦宇宙里被建构成实相，所以，用差不多相同的方式，某些意念在物质宇宙里也被建构成物质。", "en": "Their dream worlds are not then so diverse in some ways as you might have supposed. Certain symbols are constructed into realities in the dream universe, then, in much the same manner that certain ideas are constructed into matter in the physical universe.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第92节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "92", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0177", "zh": "之前我就想谈论这一点，但我们岔题了。潜意识各层的初步讨论是有必要的，因为梦源自这些不同的层面，所以应该依据它们所属的实相界域本来就有的特定象征来解读。", "en": "I had intended to cover this earlier but we were sidetracked. The preliminary discussion of the subconscious layers was necessary, since dreams originate in these various levels, and should therefore be interpreted according to the particular symbolisms inherent in the realm of reality to which they may belong.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第92节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "92", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "内我怎么挑、各层怎么读", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0216", "zh": "当我说到在一个梦的建构中使用的细腻区别，我指的是内我在选择它个人化的象征上所做的惊人工作，对很多不同的潜意识层来说，那些象征是有意义的。", "en": "When I spoke of the fine discrimination used in the construction of a dream, I had reference to the amazing work done by the inner self in the choice of its individualized symbols, which would have meaning to the many and various levels of the subconscious.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0211", "zh": "于是每一个潜意识人格就会看到和听到同一个梦，就像很多人可能看着同一部电影那样；电影院里的每一个人对剧情的象征有不同的诠释，同样的，潜意识每一层对一个梦的相同元素也有不同的诠释。", "en": "Every subconscious personality then would see and hear the same dream, as many persons may watch the same movie; and as each person in a theater interprets the symbolism of the drama differently, so does each layer of the subconscious interpret differently the same elements of one dream.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0221", "zh": "只不过在这个例子中，飞机上的那个男人，不是传送无线电讯息，而是直接用无线电，传送以编码象征呈现的一出梦戏剧，到下方那些人的心智机制内，然后下方那些人会自动转译这些象征。", "en": "Only in this case the man in the airplane, instead of a radio message, would radio directly into the mind mechanism of the men below, a dream drama in the coded symbols which would be interpreted automatically by the men below.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0255", "zh": "所以，在解读你的梦时，约瑟，和鲁柏一样，我们必须改变我们的焦点，因为透过有意识的检视而看起来合乎逻辑的解读，经常被扭曲。", "en": "In the interpretation of your dreams then Joseph, as with Ruburt's, we must change our focus, for what appears as a logical interpretation through conscious examination is often distorted.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第94节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "94", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0232", "zh": "“tub”是这个梦的统一象征，潜意识各层自有解读。在最表面的那层，做梦的鲁柏认为“有一天我会是一艘旧破船（an old tub）”的意思是一艘旧的不能再用的船（an old worn-out vessel）。这和早年的青春消失有关，而且对这个表面女性的人格有着表浅的意义。", "en": "The tub was the unifying symbol of the dream, interpreted by the various levels of the subconscious. On the most superficial level, dreaming Ruburt thought “One day I shall be an old tub,” meaning an old worn-out vessel. This having to do with the disappearance of early youth, and having superficial meaning to the surface female personality.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0233", "zh": "接着“tub”在第二层被解读成一台洗衣机，而它本身是导向下一个诠释的象征，属于一个前世，象征一艘漏水的旧破船。在他的梦中，洗衣机漏水了，把他带进第三层，这里“tub”象征一艘漏水的老爷船（old ship），在一个前世，你，约瑟，搭乘这艘船前往波士顿。", "en": "The tub was next interpreted as a washing machine in a secondary level that was in itself a symbol leading to the next interpretation, belonging to a past life, that of an old tub that leaked. In his dream the washing machine leaked, leading him into a third level, where the tub was a symbol for the old ship that leaked when you Joseph, were a passenger on your way to Boston in a past life.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第93节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "93", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B08", "title": "梦自带治疗与解题的节奏：恶梦也是你自己造的", "note": "赛斯把「治疗」写成梦的默认功能而不是附加功能。第 640 节说，不止恶梦，其他梦也遵循着一种治疗节奏，「那比任何药物都有效」，安眠药常常会干扰梦的治疗作用（同句也出现在第 795 节：安眠药阻碍梦的判断机能）。\n\n恶梦的处理办法尤其直接。第 265 节：噩梦会继续发展而把你带进恐怖之中，「除非你发觉噩梦是自行创造的。在这种情况下，它就会消失」；第 262 节补一句——如果你发觉它是你自己潜意识的产物，它们就会以噩梦消失的方式消失无踪；如果你把它当作实相，那就得应付它，直到你明白它的起因或回到普通的梦状态。第 265 节还提醒，当你从梦中的地点投射时，会遇到潜意识虚构物、别的维度但颇为有效的意象、以及在其他系统内由别人创造的建构，因为离开物质系统后你们平常的实相标准一点意义也没有。第 15 节则把恶梦放到可能性的框架里：它们代表的只是在现实中几乎不会发生的分裂可能性，也可能是主人格传给自己的讯息，警告恐惧或恐慌就在自己那层的正下方。\n\n疗愈梦可以开口要。《早期课》第 199 节记录了两个例子：第二个梦源自自我暗示，是疗愈梦的绝佳例子——「鲁柏向他的内我要求了一个疗愈梦而他收到了」；第一个声音本意原该也是一个疗愈梦，但他对自己的怀疑改变了这个梦的作用。第 200 节说那个疗愈梦缩短了两周以来的坏心情，同样的方式可以在未来被更有效地利用。第 318 节、第 321 节把它写成睡前的操作：应该睡前给自己适当的暗示，同时要求做更深一层的疗愈梦以及会提供洞见的梦，目前应该每晚都要求疗愈梦、把它当作常规（同节还顺手给了水与散步之类的小建议）。第 902 节则从另一个角度印证：你的梦以它自己的方式意味着对你进展的一个内在视像——生命之船在意识的表层之下也迅速而美丽地航行。《健康之道》第 1 节也不客气地点出，那些「负面性的」梦表现的是剩余下来的怀疑与恐惧、以及「会发生任何事件之最糟的而非最好的结果」的旧观念。\n\n梦也确实在做解题的活。第 822 节说，在那个未区分的地带充满心理上的转移与转译，直到在梦里两个自我常常彼此融合——因而你有时会带着一种短暂的兴高采烈醒来，或带着「在梦中你遇到了一个你重视的老朋友」的感觉醒来。", "src": "早期课第1册第 15 节；早期课第5册第 199、200 节；早期课第6册第 262、265 节；早期课第7册第 318、321 节；《个人与群体事件的本质》第 822 节；《梦、进化与价值完成》第 902 节；《健康之道》第 1 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 15 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 199 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 200 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 262 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 265 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 318 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 321 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 822 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 902 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 1 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "恶梦是你自己创造的，认出就散", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1230", "zh": "以略微相同的方式，噩梦会继续发展而把你带进恐怖之中，除非你发觉噩梦是自行创造的。在这种情况下，它就会消失。", "en": "In somewhat the same manner, a nightmare will run its course and lead you into terror unless you realize that the nightmare is self-created. In which instance it will disappear.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第265节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "265", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1195", "zh": "如果你发觉它是你自己潜意识的产物，它们就会以噩梦消失的那种方式消失无踪。如果你把它当作实相，那么你就得这样应付它，直到你明白它的起因，或回到普通的梦状态为止。", "en": "They will do so in the same way that a nightmare will disappear if you realize that it is a product of your own subconscious. If you treat it as a reality however, then you must deal with it as such, until you realize its origin, or return to the ordinary dream state.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第262节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "262", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1229", "zh": "你们所有人都熟悉潜意识的虚构物，因为它们存在噩梦的状况下。现在，当你从一个梦中地点投射时，你会遇到这样的潜意识意象。当你离开物质系统时，你们平常的实相标准一点意义也没有。因此你可能会同时遇到在潜意识里形成的意象；属于另一个维度但颇为有效的意象；在其他系统之内由其他人创造的建构；而且为了能有一点控制，你必须学会区分这两者。", "en": "You are familiar, all of you, with subconscious fabrications as they exist in the nightmare condition. Now on occasion when you are projecting from a dream point, you will meet such subconscious images. Your ordinary standards of reality mean absolutely nothing when you leave the physical system, therefore you will encounter, simultaneously perhaps, images that are subconsciously formed; quite valid images that belong in another dimension; constructions created by others within another system; and for any control at all, you must learn how to distinguish one from the other.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第265节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "265", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0014", "zh": "恶梦代表的只是在现实中几乎不会发生的分裂可能性。我在这里谈到恶梦，是因为我顺带提到了这个字。它们通常不像大部分的梦，是过去人格传给现在人格的讯息，不过，它们有可能是主人格传给他自己的讯息，警告他可能有恐惧或恐慌存在于主人格阶层的正下方。 这一层之下是与人格转世有关的几个阶层。再下面是处理整体种族的资料。和珍想的一样，甚至还有一些阶层是在处理人类存在之前的状态。", "en": "Nightmares represent only possibilities of disintegrations that rarely occur in actuality. I bring this up since I did mention the word in passing. They are usually not messages as most dreams are from past personalities to the present personality, though they may be messages from the primary personality to itself, as notice of fear or panic that may exist directly beneath the strata of the primary personality. Beneath this you will find the succeeding layers that have to do with personal reincarnations. Beneath this you will find material dealing with the race as a whole, and as Jane supposed even layers dealing with the prehumen state.", "source": "早期课第1册 · 第15节", "year": "1963–64", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第1册", "session": "15", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "睡前开口要：疗愈梦与洞见梦", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0819", "zh": "第二个梦源自于自我暗示，它是疗愈梦的绝佳例子。鲁柏向他的内我要求了一个疗愈梦而他收到了。", "en": "The second dream is an excellent example of a therapeutic dream that results from self-suggestion. Ruburt requested a therapeutic dream from his inner self, and he received one.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第199节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "199", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0821", "zh": "关于第一个，鲁柏至少有一点是正确的，这些声音的本意原该是一个疗愈梦。但是他自己的怀疑改变了这个梦的作用，他对他的怀疑起了反应而完全忽视了这个梦更早的一部分。", "en": "Indeed, Ruburt is correct in one respect at least, for the first dream, concerning the voices, was meant to be a therapeutic dream. However his own doubts changed the action within the dream, and he reacted to his doubts and ignored the earlier portion of the dream entirely.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第199节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "199", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0848", "zh": "疗愈梦缩短了鲁柏两周以来的坏心情，而同样的方式可以在未来更有效的被利用。", "en": "The therapeutic dream cut short Ruburts poor mood by a good two weeks, and the same tactics can be utilized in the future most productively.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第200节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "200", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1394", "zh": "一般来说，他正在作出反应的一些千里眼资料所在多有，甚至你也是。好像有一个男人，不知怎地是个隔了三层的远亲，但是与鲁柏身上的任何症状没有关联。确实应该在睡前给自己适当的暗示，同时要求自己做更深一层的疗愈梦以及会提供洞见的梦。", "en": "There is always some clairvoyant material to which he is reacting, generally speaking, even as you do. There is a man it seems, somehow three times removed, but not related with any symptoms on Ruburt’s part. Proper suggestions should indeed be given before bed, along with the request for further therapeutic dreams and dreams that will give insight.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第318节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "318", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1396", "zh": "你看，随着鲁柏允许它自己自发，再一次，他对母亲的认同将会自动消失，它引发的症状也会解除。给你们两位我最好的祝福，而且我时不时会来照看你们一下。睡前给自己建设性的暗示特别重要，并且要求自己做疗愈梦，因为这些会自动缓解早晨的症状，进一步驱散他对母亲挥之不去的认同。", "en": "You see, as Ruburt allows himself spontaneity, again, the mother identification will automatically vanish, and with it the symptoms it causes. My best wishes to you both and I shall look in on you now and then. The constructive suggestions before bed are particularly important, and the request for therapeutic dreams, as these automatically relieve the morning symptoms, and further dissipate the lingering mother identification.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第318节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "318", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1400", "zh": "水具有疗愈作用，如果可行，沿着河边散步是有益的。目前应该每晚都要求做疗愈梦，把它当作常规。一个小提示，这里不一定是一个建议：改变发色会让任何挥之不去的母亲认同减到最低。", "en": "Water has healing qualities, and walks by the river when practical, will be of benefit. Therapeutic dreams should be requested each night for now, as standard practice. A small note, not necessarily a recommendation here: a change of hair color would minimize any lingering mother identification.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第321节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "321", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "梦在替你推进与融合", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2745", "zh": "在此，你的梦以它自己的方式也符合这一点，你明白吗？因为那生命之船在意识的表层之下也迅速而美丽的航行，旅游过心灵的海水……你在表面下的层面进步得非常快。阻碍并不多，可以说，你畅行无阻，而那梦的确意味着对你的进展的一个内在视像。", "en": "Your dream3 fits in here in its own fashion, for you see that the ship of life, so to speak, rides very swiftly and beautifully also beneath the conscious surface, traveling through the waters of the psyche…. You are progressing very well at under-the-surface levels. There were few impediments. You had clear sailing, so to speak, and the dream was indeed meant as an inner vision of your progress.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第902节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "902", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2932", "zh": "你俩“负面性的”梦，都表现了剩余下来的怀疑和恐惧，以及，会发生任何事件之最糟的而非最好的结果的旧观念。鲁柏眼睛的运作，以及他视力的持续改变，指示了另外一种的进步，发生在循环系统及身体的其他部分。眼睛，知道他现在想阅读的意图，就阅读。理性并不需要告诉他如何去做。", "en": "Both of your “negative” dreams express left-over doubts and fears, and the old concept that the poorest rather than the best outcome of any event will happen. The working of Ruburt’s eyes, and the continuous changes in his vision, give indications of the other kind of improvements, happening in the circulatory systems and other portions of the body. The eyes, knowing his intent now to read, read. Reason does not have to tell him how to do this.", "source": "健康之道 · 第1节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "1", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2557", "zh": "以那种说法，这未区分的地带实际上是充满了活动的，在那儿做出了心理上的转移与转译，直到在梦里这两个自我常常彼此融合起来——因而，你有时会带着一种短暂的兴高采烈的感觉醒来，或是带着在梦中你遇到了一个你重视的老朋友的感觉醒来。", "en": "In those terms the undifferentiated area is actually filled with motion as psychological transitions and translations are made, until in dreams the two egos often merge into each other — so that sometimes you waken briefly with a sense of elation, or a feeling that in dreams you have met an old and valued friend.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第822节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "822", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B09", "title": "梦里你会遇到别人：老朋友、去世的人、以及共同的梦", "note": "梦不是独角戏。第 326 节先给分类：在你的梦中有些人你会认得是真人，他人也在投射中；有些人则是死后犹存的人格；根据你自己的投射程度，还有些人在其他层面或系统里过着正常的生活，他们可能感知你，也可能不。第 540 节说，有些已过世的亲朋的确会来探访你，由他们的世界向你的世界投射，原则上你看不到也不知道他们的形相——但你投向他们的形象，并不比他们投向你的更像「鬼」。第 551 节补一句长线：往世中很密切的朋友，如果他处在能这样做的情况下，当你在梦境时会常常与你沟通，你们的关系仍在继续，虽然你意识上不觉得。第 758 节把常被当成不现实的那类证据反过来讲：在梦里你是如此「愚蠢」，以致相信生者和死者之间有交流；你是如此「不现实」，以致仿佛造访了久已拆除的老房子，或旅行到一个你实际上从未去过的奇异外国城市——可也正是在梦里，你不觉得你自己被关在时空的匣子里，「你感觉好像所有的无限都在等着你去招呼」。\n\n你和活人也在梦里共事。第 264 节：当鲁柏梦见他和你讨论过一个梦时，大多数情况下他确实这样做了，你们两人在另一种形式里很自然地交谈；经由足够的训练，这些对话可以记录在你们两人的梦中。第 481 节说你们俩常常不知不觉从梦的状态里一同旅行，同时试着投射来做这类实验没有任何办不到的理由。第 454 节留了一句安抚：在梦里你已经建立了某些你自己并不记得的接触，等你准备好了就会记起来，并且会记起你本来就认识他们。第 195 节则指出更普遍的情形——做梦者 A 会以心电感应捕捉到做梦者 B 梦境里一小部分但很特别的内容，哪怕两人并无情感连结。第 908 节说得更整齐：你以自己视角做的梦里，另一个转世的自己也许从他的视角有同样的梦，你在其中只扮演次要角色，反之亦然。第 259 节举了历史上的实例：福克斯先生的梦中聚会很有效力，参与的人必须具备把醒时意识带入梦状态并在其中操纵的能力。\n\n最容易被忽略的是集体梦。第 253 节：你们的物质宇宙显然由共同的感知组成，集体梦当然具有相同性质，「集体梦的确是实相」，它们主要存在一个与你们的世界画面有关的特定层面。第 935 节把这条展开：你们一定会有许多较大的梦构造，只能被称为集体梦——你们自己的梦在其中发生并参与其中；你期待物质世界所有的素质彼此契合成某种永久性与秩序，同样的契合也包括主观的生活。第 933 节因此说，你的梦也是一种社会事件，做梦的状态几乎可被想成一个内在的公共论坛，每个人都可以发言，每个意见不论多不受欢迎都会被纳入考虑；当你替自己做梦时，到某程度你也替你的家庭、替更大的团体做梦。第 903 节给了这个架构的入口：一个时候你可以有不只一个梦，也可以体验可能自己的梦的版本，但总会有一些接触点；人们所有的梦形成一个集体的梦架构，世界的行动是在集体的梦沟通里解决的，那同时既是公共的又是私人的。第 891 节甚至把一个具体的政治事件读成「物质化了的群体的梦」。\n\n还有那一类被称作「说法者」的访客：第 570 节说，说法者帮助你构成梦，它们是多次元的艺术产品，其效果切开意识的各个阶段，在其中生者和死者都能参与；潜意识要比自我所知的多得多，因为自我最多只接收梦的残渣，而说法者可以在人的梦中以古人、先知、老友或其他令人信服的身份出现（《时空之外》第 570 节）。出体或有意识的投射时还会碰到另一种感知：第 900 节说，有些人在出体经验里看见比任何实质颜色更眩目的颜色，而你在梦里也看到同类颜色——那是内在感官感知的较大光谱的一部分，在梦境你根本不依赖肉体感官。", "src": "早期课第4册第 195 节；早期课第6册第 253、259、264 节；早期课第7册第 326 节；早期课第9册第 454、481 节；《灵魂永生》第 551、570 节；《时空之外》第 540、570 节；《心灵的本质》第 758 节；《梦、进化与价值完成》第 891、900、903、908、933、935 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 195 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 253 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 259 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 264 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 326 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 454 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 481 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 551 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 570 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 540 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 758 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 891 节"}, {"n": 13, "id": null, "label": "第 900 节"}, {"n": 14, "id": null, "label": "第 903 节"}, {"n": 15, "id": null, "label": "第 908 节"}, {"n": 16, "id": null, "label": "第 933 节"}, {"n": 17, "id": null, "label": "第 935 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "亡者与还在的人", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1852", "zh": "有些已过世的亲朋的确会来探访你，他们由他们的世界中向你的世界投射，原则上你看不到也不知道他们的形相。不过若是以你在梦中向他们的世界所投射出的形象与他们投向你的影像相比较，他们所投射到你们这个世界的形象并不比你投向他们的更像“鬼”。", "en": "Some dead friends and relatives do visit you, projecting from their own level of reality into yours, but you cannot as a rule perceive their forms. They are not more ghostly, or “dead,” however, than you are when you project into their reality — as you do, from the sleep state.", "source": "时空之外 · 第540节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "540", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1854", "zh": "还有些人在兜完了整个轮回的圈子之后，再决定回到这个圈子中当众生导师，在这些个案中，这些人较高层次的自我总能在某种程度上有所显现而与众不同。至于那些尚在踌躇未决的人，就会到一个过渡性的容身之地。相对上说起来，那儿是通往各世界的中途站，也可以说是一个休息站，各种亲友之间的通讯及此世与彼岸之间的交通也大部分发生在此处，而这个层面也是世间的人通常在梦中常来拜访的地方。", "en": "Some, finished with reincarnation, may choose to reenter the cycle acting as teachers, and in such cases some recognition of higher identity is always present. Now there is an in-between stage of relative indecision, a midplane of existence; a rest area, comparatively speaking, and it is from this area that most communication from relatives occurs. This is usually the level that is visited by the living in projections from the dream state.", "source": "时空之外 · 第540节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "540", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1736", "zh": "有些“坚仞的灵魂”在物质实相中活得很健旺，而他们可能有困难去适应其他非物质的活动区域。可是，在所有的这些区域中，深刻的心灵上或情感上的情况永远不会被否定。在往世中很密切的朋友，如果他处在能这样做的情况下，当你在梦境时会常常与你沟通，而你们的关系仍然在继续，虽然你意识上不觉得。", "en": "There are “hardy souls” who thrive in physical reality, and who may have difficulties acclimating to other nonphysical areas of activity. In all of these areas, however, deep spiritual or emotional contexts are never negated. Very close friends from past lives, who are in a position to do so, often communicate with you when you are in the dream state, and the relationships are continued though you do not realize it consciously.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第551节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "551", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1420", "zh": "在你的梦中，有些人将会是你认识的真人，他人也在投射中。其他人则是死后犹存的人格。在 某些情况下，根据你自己的投射程度，有些人会在其他层面或系统之内过着正常的生活。他们可能会也可能不会感知你。", "en": "Some of the people in your dreams will be actual people you know who are also projecting. Others will he survival personalities. In some cases according to your own level of projection, some will be going about their normal lives within other planes or systems. They may or may not perceive you.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第326节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "326", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2287", "zh": "在梦中，你是如此「愚蠢」，以致相信生者和死者之间有交流。你是如此（非理性），以致你想象有时在对自己死的父母说话。你是如此「不现实」，以致你仿佛造访了久已拆除的老房子，或你旅行到一个你实际上从未去过的奇异的外国城市。在梦中，你是如此的「疯狂」，以致你不觉得你自己被关在时空的匣子里。反之，你感觉好像所有的无限都在等着你去招呼。", "en": "In dreams you are so “dumb” that you believe there is a commerce between the living and the dead. You are so “irrational” as to imagine that you sometimes speak to parents who are dead. You are so “unrealistic” that it seems to you that you visit old houses, long ago torn down, or that you travel in exotic foreign cities that you have actually never visited. In dreams you are so “insane” that you do not feel yourself locked in a closet of time and space, but feel instead as if all infinity but waited your beckoning.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第758节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "758", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "你们在梦里一同旅行", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1218", "zh": "当鲁柏梦见他和你讨论过一个梦时，大多数情况下他确实是这样做了。你们两人在第二种形式中很自然地交谈了。经由足够的训练，这些对话可以记录在你们两人的梦中。必要的工作量确实是惊人的，但我告诉你，你们两人都可以这样做；你可以透过你的工作，大致了解梦状态的真实潜力。", "en": "Now when Ruburt dreams that he has discussed a dream with you, in most cases he has indeed done so. You have both conversed quite naturally in forms number two. With enough training, these conversations can be recorded in both of your dreams. The amount of work necessary is literally astounding, but I tell you that you can both do this; and you can make general, through your work, a knowledge of the true potential of the dream state.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第264节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "264", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1583", "zh": "你们是以平常的方式感知到的，这就该让你们明白：感知并不依赖物质形象。你们对你们之间的关系清清楚楚。你们俩常常在不知不觉之中，从梦的状态里以那样的方式一同旅行。你们同时试着投射来做这类实验，没有任何办不到的理由。", "en": "You perceived in a normal fashion, which should tell you that perception is not dependent upon the physical image. You were well-aware of your relationship. You have both traveled together in such a fashion often from the dream state, without your knowledge. There is no reason why you cannot try such experiments by trying to project at the same time.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第481节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "481", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1549", "zh": "在梦里你已经建立了某些你自己并不记得的接触……等你准备好了，你就会记起来……并且会记起你本来就认识他们。", "en": "Now, in your dreams you have made certain contacts that you do not recall... when you are ready you will remember... and that you have known them.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第454节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "454", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0784", "zh": "举例来说，做梦者A或许会以心电感应方式，捕捉到做梦者B梦境中一小部分但很特别的内容。做梦者A不一定跟做梦者B有情感连结，然而，最起码他跟做梦者B的梦中事件会有情感连结，因为这个事件对做梦者A有重大意义。或许某甲梦到小时候被狗咬的经验，另一个也被狗咬过的某乙，可能会以心电感应的方式捕捉到那个梦。并不是因为某乙跟这个做梦者某甲有关，而是因为这个经验会冲击到他的情感，于是他就会吸引到这个梦。", "en": "Dreamer “A” for example may pick up telepathically a small but particular portion of a dream by dreamer “B”. Dreamer “A” need not necessarily have an emotional connection with dreamer “B”. However there will be at the very least an emotional connection between the event within dreamer “B’s” dream, that has strong meaning for dreamer “A”. A man may dream of a childhood experience in which he was bitten by a dog. Another man who was also bitten by a dog, may then for example telepathically pick up the original dream. Not because he is necessarily connected with the dreamer, but because the experience will strike at him emotionally, and he will then attract the dream.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第195节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "195", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2766", "zh": "如果你正有一个梦，在其间，从你自己的视角你仍是你自己，那么，另一个转世的自己也许从他的视角有同样的梦——当然，在其中你扮演一个次要的角色。在你的梦里，那转世的自己也许出现为一个次要的角色，且相当的在你注意力的边缘。而如果那梦包括了关于一曲戏或一个发明的概念时，那么，那戏或发明可能出现为在两历史时代的一个具体事件，其程度则要看活在那时间里的那两个个人诠释资讯的能力。但世代以来，文化不只是以物质的方法传播的。能力与发明并不依赖人类的迁徙，但那些迁徙本身却是在梦里所给的资讯本的结果，那些梦告诉人类的部落在哪儿可以找到较好的家园。", "en": "If you are having a dream as yourself from your own perspective, another reincarnational self may be having the same dream from its perspective—in which, of course, you play a minor role. In your dream, that reincarnational self may appear as a minor character, quite on the periphery of your attention, and if the dream were to include an idea, say, for a play or an invention, then that play or invention might appear as a physical event in both historic times, to whatever degree it would be possible for the two individuals living in time to interpret that information. But culture throughout the ages was spread by more than physical means. Abilities and inventions were not dependent upon human migrations, but those migrations themselves were the result of information given in dreams, telling tribes of men the directions in which better homelands could be found.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第908节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "908", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1167", "zh": "例如，福克斯先生的梦中聚会就非常有效力。在这种情况下，事情就是这个样子。那些在梦实相之内同意这样一个梦中聚会的人必须具备一定的能力。他们必须能够把醒时意识带入梦状态中。他们必须能够在其中进行操纵。因此，他们每个人都建构了，你知道，他们同意见面的梦中地点，这是鲁柏的福克斯先生没想到的一点。", "en": "For example, Mr. Foxs dream meetings were quite valid. This is what happens in such a case. Those who agree on such a meeting within dream reality must have certain abilities developed. They must be capable of taking waking consciousness into the dream state. They must be able to manipulate within it. Each of them therefore constructs, you see, the dream location at which the have agreed to meet, a point not thought of by Ruburt's Mr. Fox.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第259节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "259", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "集体梦与内在的公共论坛", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1101", "zh": "你们的物质宇宙显然是由共同的感知组成的，而集体梦当然具有相同的性质。集体梦的确是实相。它们主要存在一个特定的层面，与你现在所谓的世界画面（world pictures）有关。", "en": "Now, your physical universe is obviously composed of shared perceptions, and mass dreams would of course be of the same nature. Mass dreams are indeed a reality. They exist mainly at one particular level, and they have to do with what you may call for now, world pictures.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第253节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "253", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2848", "zh": "那么，你们一定会有许多较大的梦构造（formations），那只能被称为集体梦（group dreams）——你们自己的梦在其中发生，并参与其中的主观事件。你们期待物质世界所有的素质，不论多不同，都彼此契合而形成某种的永久性及秩序。那么，这同类的“彼此契合”也包括了主观的生活——或比如说，你私下的梦也是一个更大的梦实相里的片段，应该不会令你惊奇。私人梦对那个实相之运作的重要性，就如电子对你们物质实相一样的重要（停顿良久），为智慧及乐趣的累积提供了内在的途径。", "en": "(Long pause at 8:50.) You are bound to have, then, many larger dream formations that can only be called group dreams—subjective events in which your own dreams happen, and in which your own dreams take part. You expect all of the elements of the physical world, however diverse, to fit together and form a certain kind of permanency and order. It should be no surprise, then, that this same kind of “fitting together” includes subjective life also—or that, say, your private dreams are also fragments in a vaster dream reality. They are as important to the operation of that reality as electrons are to your physical one (long pause), providing inner pathways for the accumulation of wisdom and pleasure.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第935节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "935", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2825", "zh": "然而（记住我对于似乎是的矛盾所说的话），你的梦也是一种社会事件，而做梦的状态几乎可被想作是一个内在的公共论坛，在其中每个人都可以发言，并且每个意见不论多不受欢迎都会被纳入考虑。如果你想称任何一个梦事件为一个私人事件，那么，我必须告诉你，那个私人事件实际上是，你个人对一个更大的多面及多层的梦事件之贡献。所以，其中一个层面也许与你所属的一个团体——比如说你的家庭、你的政治或宗教组织——的利益有关，进而“向外”延伸到全国性政府及世界性事件的领域。（停顿。）正如一般而言，你私人的有意识生活是住在某一种的社区环境里，所以，你的梦发生在同样的范畴里；所以当你替你自己做梦时，到某程度，你也替你的家庭、替你的社区及为全世界做梦。", "en": "Yet (remembering what I said about seeming contradictions), your dreams are also social events of a kind, and the state of dreaming can almost be thought of as an inner public forum in which each man and woman has his or her say, and in which each opinion, however unpopular, is taken into consideration. If you want to call any one dream event a private event, then I would have to tell you that that private event actually was your personal contribution to a larger multisided dream event, many-layered, so that one level might deal with the interests of a group to which you belong—say your family, [or] your political or religious organization—reaching “outward” to the realm of national government and world affairs. (Pause.) As your private conscious life is lived in a community setting of one kind or another as a rule, so do your dreams take place in the same context, so that as you dream for yourself, to some extent you also dream for your own family, for your community, and for the world.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第933节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "933", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2751", "zh": "你知道，在一个时候你可以有不只一个梦。你也可以体验可能自己的梦的版本，但总会有一些接触点——那是说，你为什么会收到这样的一个梦总有一个理由。人们所有的梦形成了一个集体的梦架构。梦存在于其他的层面，而当然，在物质层面它们影响身体的状态。以这种方式，世界的行动是在集体的梦沟通里解决的，那在同时既是公共的又是私人的。", "en": "You know you can have more than one dream at a time. You can also experience versions of dreams of probable selves, but there will always be some point of contact—that is, there will always be a reason why you pick up such a dream. All of the dreams people have form a mass dream framework. Dreams exist at other levels, and physically of course they affect the body state. In such ways, the world’s actions are worked out in mass dream communications that are at the same time public and private.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第903节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "903", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2689", "zh": "像整个的美国人质事件（在伊朗），任意一个实质事件成为一个焦点，吸引了它所有的可能版本和结果。人质的情况（现在已是第53天）是个物质化了的群体的梦，它意在将其重要性与活力显示在实相的政治和宗教舞台上，意在将信念的冲突戏剧化，并把那冲突向外投射到众所周知的领域。在人类行为最基本的层面，每个涉及的人都有意识或无意识的自愿参与，当然，1980年即刻被那事件先罩上了阴影。这世界将拿它怎么办？", "en": "Like the entire American hostage affair (in Iran), any physical event serves as a focus that attracts all of its probable versions and outcomes. The hostage situation (now in day 53) is a materialized mass dream, meant to be important and vital on political and religious platforms of reality, meant to dramatize a conflict of beliefs, and to project that conflict outward into the realm of public knowledge. Everyone involved was consciously and unconsciously a willing participant at the most basic levels of human behavior, and it is of course no coincidence that 1980 is immediately foreshadowed by that event. What will the world do with it?", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第891节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "891", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "说法者与其他感官", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1778", "zh": "“说法者”帮助你构成梦,它们的确可算是一种多次元的艺术产品——存在于不止一个实相的梦，其效果切开了意识的各个不同的阶段，以你们的话来说，那些阶段对生者和死者都是真的，而在其中生者和死者都能参与。因此之故，灵感与启示才会如此常是梦的一部分。", "en": "The Speakers help you in the formation of dreams which are indeed multidimensional artistic productions of a kind — dreams existing in more than one reality, with effects that dissect various stages of consciousness that are real, in your terms, to both the living and the dead and in which both the living and the dead may participate. It is for this reason that inspirations and revelations are so often a part of the dream condition.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第570节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "570", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1860", "zh": "无意识或潜意识要比你的“自我”部分所知的多得多，因为“自我”所能接收到的最多只是梦的残渣。说法者可以以古人、先知、老友或其他令人信服的身份在人的梦中出现。", "en": "The unconscious and subconscious areas, however, are aware of much more of this information than the ego, for it receives only the minute residue of dream material as a rule. The Speakers therefore may appear within dreams as historical characters, as prophets, as trusted old friends, or in whatever guise will impress the particular personality.", "source": "时空之外 · 第570节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "570", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2729", "zh": "现在：在某些场合，有时在濒临死亡时，但常常只在有意识的出体情况，人可以感知那种光。举例来说，在有些出体经验里，鲁柏看见比任何实质的颜色更为眩目的颜色，而你在梦里看到同类的颜色。它们是你内在感官所感知的较大光谱的一部分，而在梦境中你根本不依赖你的肉体感官。", "en": "Now: On certain occasions, sometimes near the point of death, but often simply in conscious states outside of the body, man is able to perceive that kind of light. In some out-of-body experiences Ruburt, for example, saw colors more dazzling than any physical ones, and you saw the same kind of colors in your dream. They are a part of your inner senses’ larger spectrum of perception, and in the dream state you were not relying upon your physical senses at all.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第900节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "900", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B10", "title": "可以主动跟梦打交道：睡前给暗示，练到醒着也知道", "note": "这一页最后落到那我今晚能做什么。赛斯给的第一个动作是睡前一句话。《未知的实相》卷二第 719 节给的是一个很好上手的心理技巧：睡觉前告诉你自己，你会把那晚最重要的梦在脑海里「照一张梦的照片」，想象你带着一部照相机进入梦境，在最清晰的感知点按下快门，再把它带回来——醒来时它是你脑海里看见的第一张画面。第 497 节把频率也说清了：把这养成习惯，每周两个晚上给出这些暗示，终究会极为成功，而且建议把那两个晚上连着安排。第 325 节甚至允许你暗示得更大一点——连同梦暗示，暗示自己会在梦中投射并看见赛斯，而他会告诉你他是他，这样你就知道是他。第 206 节预告了这条路的方向：将会有建议让你们可以引导梦中的自己从事不同的活动、参访某些地方、带回来某些讯息，这些「蕴含在人类人格的能力和你自己能力范围里」。\n\n第二个动作是记录与追下去。第 87 节直接对当事人说：你会发现分析你的梦将会促进你的内在发展，也会让你比较容易在外在世界走出你的路——如果可能，我建议你追踪你的梦。第 499 节给了一个为期一周的暖身：用梦的回忆来为实验做准备，有几个晚上观想自己置身于给过的梦环境里，入睡时给自己暗示。第 211 节说这些实验所涉及的工作本身就会让你们变得十分有弹性，最后你会发现睡觉时带着自己一部分的意识。第 782 节则像一句收尾的口谕：既然我告诉了你所有如何去实行的说法，那么我祝你在今晚的梦中有最令人兴奋的经验。\n\n第三个动作是难度较高的：在梦里知道自己在梦里。第 275 节给的识别法很实用——从它们生动至极的样子认出那些一开始你以为自己醒着的梦，「记住这一点就够了，这样在做梦时，你就能够发觉它是什么」。第 269 节解释为什么这算一条通路：当你能使用批判能力，你就晓得自己不在平常的醒时状态；在看似醒着的梦中你确实是醒着的，只是在一个不同的心理与实相架构之内（同册第 246 节还留着一句期待）。第 652 节说，当你发现自己在梦境与醒时一样警觉、有反应、而且理性，你就不可能在旧架构中继续运作；第 575 节说熟练之后你不再身不由主地被各种意识状态卷着走，而是自己知道、也能自己控制——意识是一种可以被转移到各种方向的工具，它不等于你，只是属于你的东西，而你正在学习怎么运用它。第 246 节赛斯自己等的那一天是：你们觉知你们自己的梦，但同时又是清醒的，充分意识到物质环境，并能在物质宇宙中正常运作。\n\n再往远处看，这件事是有分量的活。第 723 节说，你可以决定借由利用你的做梦活动去扩大你的世界观，书里给的许多练习就是朝那个方向设计；第 700 节称真正的做梦艺术是一个久已被你们的世界遗忘的科学，追求它会训练你的脑子有一种新的意识——在两种存在里都同样自在、同样安全；几乎任何人都可以成为一个令人满意且有生产力的业余玩家，但真正的成就需要多年训练、强烈的目的感与一种奉献。第 173 节给了一句衡量标准：人格的稳定性，或多或少取决于它掌握或操控这些梦境的效能。", "src": "早期课第3册第 87 节；早期课第4册第 173 节；早期课第5册第 206、211 节；早期课第6册第 246、269、275 节；早期课第7册第 325 节；早期课第9册第 497、499 节；《未知的实相卷一》第 700 节；《未知的实相卷二》第 719、723 节；《个人实相的本质》第 652 节；《时空之外》第 575 节；《心灵的本质》第 782 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 87 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 173 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 206 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 211 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 246 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 269 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 275 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 325 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 497 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 499 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 700 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 719 节"}, {"n": 13, "id": null, "label": "第 723 节"}, {"n": 14, "id": null, "label": "第 652 节"}, {"n": 15, "id": null, "label": "第 575 节"}, {"n": 16, "id": null, "label": "第 782 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "睡前的那一句暗示", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2144", "zh": "在睡觉以前告诉你自己，你会把那晚最重要的梦在脑海里照一张梦的照片。告诉你自己，你甚至会在睡觉时觉察到你在这样做，而想象你带着一部照相机进入梦境，在你最清晰的感知点你用这相机照你的照片，而——再次在脑海里——把它带回来。所以，当你醒来时，它会是你脑海里看见的第一张画面。", "en": "Now this is a mental camera we are using. There is a knack about being a good dream photographer, and you must learn how to operate the camera. In physical life, for example, a photographer knows that many conditions affect the picture he takes. Exterior situations then are important: You might get a very poor picture on a dark day, for instance. With our dream camera, however, the conditions themselves are mental. If you are in a dark mood, for example, then your picture of inner reality might be dim, poorly outlined, or foreboding. This would not necessarily mean that the dream itself had tragic overtones, simply that it was taken in the “poor light” of the psyche’s mood. (Pause at 11:40.) Inner weather changes constantly, even as the exterior weather does. One dream picture with a dreary cast, therefore, is not much different from a physical photograph taken on a rainy afternoon.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第719节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "719", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1606", "zh": "你们若把这养成习惯——每周两个晚上给出这些暗示——终究会极为成功。我建议把那两个晚上连着安排。鲁柏应当在这两晚入睡时试用他的方法之一；倘若不成，让他使用他从梦境状态中会用的其他方法。", "en": "Now if you make a habit of doing this, of giving these suggestions two evenings a week, you will ultimately be highly successful. I suggest that the two nights be together. Ruburt should try on these nights one of his methods as he falls asleep, and if that does not succeed, tell him to use the other methods that he knows from the dream state.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第497节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "497", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1411", "zh": "连同你的梦暗示，你可以暗示你会在梦中投射看见我，而我会告诉你，我是我，这样你就会知道是我。", "en": "You may, along with your dream suggestions, suggest that you see me on a dream projection, and I will tell you that I am I so you will know me.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第325节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "325", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0900", "zh": "首先一个小提醒。过一阵子将会有其他的建议给你们，让你们可以引导梦中的自己在梦中从事不同的活动，参访某些地方、带回来某些讯息等等。这明显的还是很未来的事，但它蕴含在人类人格的能力和你自己能力范围里。", "en": "First here a small note. Much later there will be other suggestions for you, in which you will direct your sleeping self to perform various activities in sleep, visit certain locations, bring back information and so forth. This is obviously still very much in the future, but it is well within the abilities of human personality, and within the realms of your own abilities.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第206节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "206", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "记下来、追下去", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e0155", "zh": "我不会再延长这一节的时间。你会发现分析你的梦将会促进你的内在发展，也会让你比较容易在外在世界走出你的路。约瑟，如果可能的话，我建议你追踪你的梦；你想要分析的梦，就在上课前把它们的副本放在桌子上。", "en": "I will not extend this session for any length of time. You will find that an analysis of your dreams will further your inner development, and also make your way easier in the outer world. I suggest you keep track of your dreams, Joseph, when possible; and that copies of dreams for which you would like analysis be left on the table before sessions.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第87节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "87", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0937", "zh": "你们看，这些实验所涉及的工作本身就会让你们变得十分有弹性，而最后你会发现，当你睡觉的时候会带着你自己一部分的意识。我们也应该让你运用暗示去带出某种我们可能会感兴趣的梦。", "en": "You see, the work involved in these experiments will of itself allow you to be extremely flexible, and you will discover finally that you can bring a portion of your consciousness with you while you sleep. A portion of the self will be investigating other portions of the self. We shall also have you using suggestion to bring about certain types of dreams in which we may be interested.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第211节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "211", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1629", "zh": "给我们一会儿时间，约瑟。（长停顿。）我告诉过你，用一个星期的时间，通过梦的回忆来为你的实验做准备。有几个晚上，观想你自己置身于我给过的鲁柏的梦的环境里。当你入睡时，给你自己一个暗示：你和他开始在沙上走。看见你们两个在一起，而且手拉着手。现在，鲁柏也该做同样的练习。", "en": ", Joseph. (Long pause.) I told you to take a week to prepare yourself through dream recall for your experiments. For several evenings, see yourself in the environment of Ruburts dream as given. As you fall asleep give yourself the suggestion that you and he begin to walk upon the sand. See the both of you together, and hand in hand. Now Ruburt should do the same exercise.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第499节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "499", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2349", "zh": "口授完毕。给你们我最衷心的问候。既然我告诉了你所有如何去实行的说法，那么我祝你在今晚的梦中有最令人兴奋的经验（觉得有趣地）。", "en": "End of session. My heartiest regards. I wish you exciting experiences in the dreams you will have tonight, now that I’m telling you all about how to do it (amused).", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第782节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "782", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "练到醒着也知道自己在做梦", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e1276", "zh": "我们的训练将从利用这些梦开始，不管它们发生在何时。你从它们生动至极的样子就认得出它们。这些就是一开始你确定你醒着的梦。记住这一点就够了，这样在做梦时，你就能够发觉它是什么。", "en": "Our instructions will begin with the use of these dreams whenever they occur. You will know them by their intense vividness. These are dreams when at first you are certain that you are awake. It is sufficient to keep this in mind, so that in the middle of a dream you can realize what it is.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第275节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "275", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1243", "zh": "现在。关于你感觉自己确实醒着的梦，我想给你一些注。当这些梦异常生动时，自我对参与是有觉知的。然而，一般来说，自我并没有在使用它的批判能力。", "en": "Now. There were some notes I wanted to give you concerning dreams in which you feel certain that you are awake. When these dreams are unusually vivid, then the ego is aware of participating. The ego is not using its critical faculties however as a general rule.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第269节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "269", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1244", "zh": "你明白，你可以使用批判能力。但是你看，当你这样做的时候，你晓得你就不是在平常的醒时状态。在看似醒着的梦中，你确实是醒着的，当然是在一个不同的心理架构之内。甚至，是在一个不同的实相架构之内。", "en": "As you realize, you can use the critical faculties. But you see when you do so you realize that you are not in your normal waking condition. In awake-seeming dreams you are indeed awake, within of course a different psychological framework. Indeed, within a different framework of reality.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第269节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "269", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1087", "zh": "我还在等那一天的到来，你们觉知你们自己的梦，但同时又是清醒的，充分意识到你们自己的物质环境，同时能够在物质宇宙中正常运作的那一天。", "en": "I am still waiting for the day when you become aware of your own dreams while you are awake, and conscious at the same time, fully, of your physical environment, and able to operate normally at the same time in the physical universe.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第246节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "246", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1924", "zh": "当你发现，你在梦境与醒时一样地警觉、有反应，而且理性，你就不可能在旧架构中继续运作了。这并不是指在所有的梦中，你都能达到那种觉察，但在我建议的醒睡模式之内，这种情形可以常常被达到。", "en": "When you find yourself as alert, responsive, and intellectual in the dream state as you are in waking life, it becomes impossible to operate within the old framework. This does not mean that in all dreams that particular kind of awareness is achieved, but it is often accomplished within the suggested wake-sleep pattern.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第652节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "652", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1866", "zh": "当你在这方面熟练之后，你不再会身不由主的在睡梦中颠倒于各种意识状态之中，你会自己知道，也能自己控制这种种活动。“意识”是“灵魂”特有的一种东西，是一种可以被转移到各种方向的“工具”。“意识”并不等于“你”，“你”也不是它；它只是属于你的、属于你灵魂的一种东西。而你，正在学习怎么样去运用自己的意识。在你对各种意识的面貌有了相当程度的了解，而又能加以运用时，你就能对自己的世界有更多的了解。那时候，“意识的我”才真正会回归到真正“意识”应处的地位。", "en": "When you are proficient you will not be swept willy-nilly into other stages of consciousness as you sleep, but will be able to understand and direct these activities. Consciousness is an attribute of the soul, a tool that can be turned in many directions. You are not your consciousness. It is something that belongs to you and to the soul. You are learning to use it. To the extent that you understand and utilize the various aspects of consciousness, you will learn to understand your own reality, and the conscious self will truly become conscious.", "source": "时空之外 · 第575节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "575", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "把做梦当成一门可以长的功夫", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2206", "zh": "在梦境里，你涉足到你醒时的世界观之外，你能把其它的兴趣与活动带入焦点。这些在醒时可以留在背景里——或你可以决定借由利用你的做梦活动去扩大你的世界观。在这儿给的许多练习是朝着那个方向设计的。", "en": "In the dream state you range beyond your waking world view. You are able to bring into focus other interests and activities. These can remain in the background during waking life — or you can decide to enlarge your world view by taking advantage of your dreaming activities. Many of the exercises given here are geared in that direction.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第723节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "723", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2080", "zh": "真正的做梦艺术是一个久已被你们的世界遗忘了的科学。当你去追求这样一种艺术时，就会训练你的脑子有一种新的意识——那是在两种存在里都同样自在，都同样安住和安全的一种意识。在这种艺术——科学里，几乎任何人都可以变成一个令人满意，而且具生产力的业余玩家，但其真正的成就需要多年的训练、一种强烈的目的感及一种奉献热忱——就与任何真正的天职没有两样。", "en": "The true art of dreaming is a science long forgotten by your world.1 Such an art, pursued, trains the mind in a new kind of consciousness — one that is equally at home in either existence, well-grounded and secure in each. Almost anyone can become a satisfied and productive amateur in this art-science; but its true fulfillment takes years of training, a strong sense of purpose, and a dedication — as does any true vocation.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第700节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "700", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0603", "zh": "它一直都在这个架构里面，但并不是永远都在里面维持同样的相对位置。人格的稳定性或多或少取决于它掌握或操控这些梦境的效能。人格会在物质宇宙之内操控，这一点众所周知，不过人格在梦宇宙之内也一定会做类似的操控，这一点大家普遍都不知道。", "en": "It is always within this framework, but not always does it maintain the same relative position within it. The stability of the personality depends in some degree upon the effectiveness with which it handles and manipulates these dream situations. It is realized that the personality manipulates within the physical universe, but the fact is not generally accepted that similar manipulations must be made within this dream universe.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第173节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "173", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B11", "title": "梦与文明：人是先梦过，再造出他所知的世界", "note": "最后一页把镜头拉远：梦不只关乎你昨晚睡了几个小时。《梦、进化与价值完成》第 889 节给了一个起点——以你们的时间来说会有个开始，「在开始是早期人类的梦容许他能应付物质实相的。梦的世界是他最初的学习园地」：干旱时他梦到水源所在，饥荒时梦到食物所在，他的梦让他以千里眼的方式看到大块陆地，因此不必在尝试错误上浪费时间。同节说，在那些早先的时代所有物种以一种对现在人类相当无意识的方式共享他们的梦，人在梦中向动物探询——远在他学会跟踪动物之前；人们互通各自的位置、文化与了解的象征、艺术的性质，从第一件工具到火的发现，所有那些发明都是梦世界的灵感和通讯的结果：「人梦到他的世界，而后创造它」\n\n第 890 节把这条账记到文明头上：你们所有的制成品都源自梦的领域，最先是被抽象地构思；有关人那些尚未发展能力的暗示，永远在梦境、在艺术、宗教甚至科学里出现，在政治与商业里则作为大半被忽略的直觉背景。同节两句最能概括这一页：「世界的真实历史即为人的梦的历史」，以及梦对农业的发展负责、对工业、对国家的兴亡、对罗马的光荣与毁灭也一样，连你们目前的科技进步都可以几乎直溯到印刷术与爱迪生的发明——那是梦中灵感的直接显现。第 892 节给这句一个理由：你们为自己「设定了时间」，但更广大的感知总是出现在你们意识和梦境的背后，「是梦境的伟大活动使你们，作为心理和肉体的生物，可以去认知和居住于你们所知的世界」。\n\n语言也在这条线上。第 893 节说，按照你们的时间尺度，有很长很长的一段时间，人在梦境远比醒来的时间长，那是一个如梦般却充满活力、想象力在试玩所有可能性的世界；「人梦到他的语言。他梦到如何用他的舌头形成字句」，他练习把字符串起来以产生意义——所有的语言都以在梦中所说的语言为基础，而当人较少是个做梦者、较多沉浸在特定的时刻里时，语言的需要才升起。同节说，这些古老的梦为每一个从事尘世冒险的意识所分享，河谷与山峦及其居民「一同把他们自己梦进了存在与共生状态」。第 912 节则给这套机制一个先天层：基因性的梦直接被基因的启动所激发，有助于形成并指导出生前的意识，终你一生以某种形式持续；它们让人去因觅食而迁徙、领他去肥沃之地，在生存仿佛受威胁时会尽可能升到意识层面——「基因性的梦是预警饥荒或战争的梦」。同节也说，运用语言的能力是基因性地生成、由父母的母语实质启动的，儿童早在实际说出那种语言之前就已经在梦里形成它。\n\n给初学者的两个具体收尾。第 901 节说，在那开始时梦游一度远比现在普遍，其中「内我真的教肉体走路」，以免新冒出的、肉体取向的理性问太多问题、挡住身体平顺的自发性移动；第 904 节说，人类心灵的内在转世结构对肉体的存活非常重要，因而他们能梦见前生而记起比如说如何走路或说话，被他们随后忘掉的记忆所导引。第 911 节还留了一句短到可以背下来的话：做梦的状态提供了在这些意识系统之间的连接的环节（同节也说，梦提供你们另一种宇宙性的语言，一个不论物质环境、国籍或联盟而多少统一了所有人的语言）。至于今天：第 867 节说，光靠知性，人根本无法从每日经验里学会一个季节跟着另一个季节，因为他太活在当下——可是他在一个季节里梦到其他的季节，梦见自己散播果实的种子；《神奇之道》第 17 节说，早期的画家画画是为了与人分享他们在梦中看到的影像，他们在睡眠中练习做梦，因此在真的移动物体之前，先在心中把物体移来移去；而你们此刻的发明冲动也可能只是被「记忆中自己在梦中飞行」的模糊印象所触发（《时空之外》第 583 节）。最后，《个人与群体事件的本质》第 844 节把核子动力这类力量放回它的位置：它以属于上帝的样子站在人的梦里——那是宇宙的力量。", "src": "早期课第3册第 109 节；《时空之外》第 583 节；《个人与群体事件的本质》第 844、867 节；《梦、进化与价值完成》第 889、890、892、893、901、904、911、912 节；《神奇之道》第 17 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 109 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 583 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 844 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 867 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 889 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 890 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 892 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 893 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 901 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 904 节"}, {"n": 11, "id": null, "label": "第 911 节"}, {"n": 12, "id": null, "label": "第 912 节"}, {"n": 13, "id": null, "label": "第 17 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "梦的世界是人最初的学习园地", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2677", "zh": "可是，以你们的时间来说，我们将说到一个开始，而在开始是早期人类的梦容许他能应付物质实相的。梦的世界是他最初的学习园地。在干旱时他会梦到水源所在；在饥荒时他会梦到食物所在。也就是，他的梦让他可以通过千里眼方式看到大块陆地。他不会在你们现在视为理所当然的尝试错误的过程上浪费时间。在梦里他的意识以波状运作。", "en": "In your terms of time, however, we will speak of a beginning, and in that beginning it was early man’s dreams that allowed him to cope with physical reality. The dream world was his original learning ground. In times of drought he would dream of the location of water. In times of famine he would dream of the location of food. That is, his dreaming allowed him to clairvoyantly view the body of land. He would not waste time in the trial-and-error procedures that you now take for granted. In dreams his consciousness operated as a wave.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第889节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "889", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2678", "zh": "在那些早先的时代，所有的物种以一种对现在人类而言相当无意识的方式共享他们的梦，因此在梦中人也向动物探询——远在他学到跟踪动物之前。食物或水在哪儿？那块地的方位如何？人类探索这行星，因为他的梦告诉他陆地在那儿。", "en": "In those early times all species shared their dreams in a way that is now quite unconscious for your kind, so that in dreams man inquired of the animals also—long before he learned to follow the animal tracks, for example. Where is there food or water? What is the lay of the land? Man explored the planet because his dreams told him that the land was there.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第889节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "889", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2679", "zh": "那时人们不是像现在看起来那么孤立，因为早期人类在他们的梦里互通他们各自的位置、他们文化和了解的象征、及他们艺术的性质。你们现在常以为颇为偶然的所有发明——从第一件工具到火的重要性的发现、或铁器时代或不论什么的来临——所有的那些发明，即为梦世界的灵感和通讯的结果。人梦到他的世界，而后创造它，而意识单位首先梦到人和所有你们所知的其它物种。", "en": "People were not nearly as isolated as it now appears, for in their dreams early men communicated their various locations, the symbols of their cultures and understanding, the nature of their arts. All of the inventions that you often think now happened quite by chance—the discovery of anything from the first tool to the importance of fire, or the coming of the Iron Age or whatever—all of that inventiveness was the result of the inspiration and communication of the dream world. Man dreamed his world and then created it, and the units of consciousness first dreamed man and all of the other species that you know.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第889节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "889", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2617", "zh": "光是运用知性的话，世代以来人根本无法借由每日经验学到，好比说，一个季节是跟在另一个季节之后，因为他会太活在当下而无法做到那点。可是，在一个季节里，他梦到其他的季节，而在梦里他看见自己散播水果的种子，就如他在日常生活里看到风在播种一样。", "en": "Using the intellect alone, man did not simply learn through daily experience over the generations, say, that one season followed the other. He lived too much in the moment for that. In one season he dreamed of the others, however, and in dreams he saw himself spreading the seeds of fruits as he had seen the wind do in daily life.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第867节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "867", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2917", "zh": "当人类需要某种才能时，它们便升到前方来，就如鲁柏现在的例子。当你在画画时，你也在转译内在知识。早期的画家画画以与人分享他们在梦中看到的影像。换句话说，他们在睡眠中练习做梦，因而也学会以物体的影像尺寸来想，而在他们真的移动物体之前，在他们心中将物体移来移去。", "en": "When the species needs certain abilities, they rise to the fore, as in the case of Ruburt now. When you are painting pictures you are also translating inner knowledge. Early artists drew pictures to share the images they saw in their dreams. In a fashion they practiced dreaming in their sleep, and thus learned also to think (underlined) in terms of the measurement of physical images, and to move objects around in their minds before they did so physically.", "source": "神奇之道 · 第17节", "year": "1980", "tag": null, "short": "神奇之道", "session": "17", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1868", "zh": "如果你这一生所选择的方向特别着重在载人的交通工具方面，那么你可能会受到记忆中自己在梦中飞行的模糊印象所触发，而发明出火箭或飞机等等东西。但是如果你一直注重的只是“内在”，而你又了解自己的意识本来就可在体外自在飞翔的事实，那么，想要发展出可以具体移动物体的工具那种冲动就不会这么强烈了。", "en": "If you are presently experiencing a life in which you have chosen high emphasis upon physical locomotion, for example, then through vague dream memories of flying you can be inspired toward, say, the invention of airplanes or rockets; but if you actually understand the fact that your own consciousness can indeed travel outside of the body, then the impetus toward physical developments in locomotion is not nearly so intense.", "source": "时空之外 · 第583节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "583", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "世界的真实历史即为人的梦的历史", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2685", "zh": "你们所有的制成品也是源自梦的领域，显然它们最先是被抽象的构思，而以相同的方式，人造出他最先的工具。人天生具有（停顿良久）所有那些能力——给予他特征的能力——以及那些以你们的说法尚待发展的能力。并非他到现在还没用它们，而是在你们所认为的文明之连续性主线上，他还没关注于它们。有关那些能力的暗示，永远在梦境，在艺术、宗教、甚至科学里出现。它们出现在政治与商业里，却是作为大半未显现的直觉背景，而大半被忽略。我们在此书稍后会回到这上面去。", "en": "All of your manufactured objects also originated in the realm of dreams, first obviously being conceived of mentally, and in the same way man produced his first tools. He was born with (long pause) all of those abilities—abilities by which he is now characterized—and with other abilities that in your terms still wait for development. Not that he has not used them so far, but that he has not focused upon them in what you consider the main lines of civilized continuity. Hints of those abilities are always present in the dream state, and in the arts, in the religions, and even in the sciences. They appear in politics and business, but as the largely unmanifest intuitive background, which is largely ignored. We will return to these later in the book.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第890节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "890", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2686", "zh": "人的梦一直给他一阵动力、目的及意义的感觉，并给他形成他文明的素材。世界的真实历史即为人的梦的历史，因为它们对所有的历史性发展多少都要负责。", "en": "Man’s dreams have always provided him with a sense of impetus, purpose, meaning, and given him the raw material from which to form his civilizations. The true history of the world is the history of man’s dreams, for they have been responsible in one way or another for all historic developments.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第890节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "890", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2687", "zh": "梦对农业的发展负责，对工业、对国家的兴亡、对那曾为罗马的“光荣”以及罗马的毁灭也一样。（停顿。）你们目前科技的进步，可几乎直溯到印刷术的发明，到爱迪生的发明，那是梦中灵感的直接显现。但如果我现在告诉你们是真的，那么很显然，当我说你们的物质世界是源自梦世界，我必然是指与通常对梦实相的定义非常不同的东西。再次的，我可以选择另一个用语，但我想强调每个人与那另一个实相的密切联系，那实相的确发生在你们所认作的梦境里（全都很热切的）。", "en": "They were responsible for the birth of agriculture, as well as industry, the rise and fall of nations, the “glory” that was Rome, and Rome’s destruction. (Pause.) Your present technological advances can almost be dated from the [invention of] the printing press, to Edison’s inventions, which were flashes of intuition, dream-inspired. But if what I am telling you is true, then it is obvious that when I say that your physical world originated in the world of dreams, I must mean something far different from the usual definition of dream reality. Again, I could choose another term, but I want to emphasize each person’s intimate contact with that other reality that does occur in what you think of as the state of dreaming (all very intently).", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第890节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "890", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2696", "zh": "你们为自己“设定了时间”——但更广大的感知总是出现在你们意识和梦境的背后。是梦境的伟大活动使你们，作为心理和肉体的生物，可以去认知和居住于你们所知的世界（较大声）。", "en": "You “timed” yourselves—but greater perceptions always appeared in the background of your consciousnesses and in the dream state. It is the great activity of the dream state that allows you, as psychological and physical creatures, to recognize and inhabit the world that you know (louder).", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第892节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "892", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0367", "zh": "观念、意念，在梦中实现，接着在物质上被建构出来。物质建构和发明，纯粹是就理智上对物质的一个新的理解而言，也转化了梦宇宙的本质，并且让它更丰富，增加那里在象征上可能有的自由。", "en": "Concepts, ideas, realized in dreams, have then been constructed physically. Physical constructions and inventions, purely in terms of a new intellectual comprehension of physical matter, have also transformed the nature of the dream universe, and enriched it, adding to the symbolistic freedoms possible there.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第109节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "109", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0368", "zh": "之前我提过火，人类在理智上意会到它，因此在物质的世界里让它化为具体。先前梦世界只是就它转化物质的力量，在象征上使用火。人类在更早之前不能够看见火带来有益的温暖或舒适。所以他无法，比方说，在他的梦宇宙里操纵火，除了他认为的破坏以外。", "en": "Earlier I mentioned fire, which was intellectually grasped by man and therefore materialized in the world of matter. Previously the dream world symbolically used fire only in terms of its power to transform matter. Man was not able to see fire earlier in terms of beneficial warmth or comfort. He could not for example in his dream universe then manipulate fire, except in terms of what he considered destruction.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第109节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "109", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2593", "zh": "显而易见的，核子动力代表力量。它是好还是坏？它以属于上帝的样子站在人的梦里：宇宙的力量（专注的）。以你们的说法，人一直认为自己是与自然分开的，因此，他必然觉得与自然的力量分开——而在他梦里，两者之间必然有一个非常大的分界。那么，事实上，核能以一个梦中象征的样子来到，而浮出世界里，作为一种我们要处理的东西。", "en": "Nuclear power stands for power, plain and simple. Is it good or bad? It stands in man’s dreams as belonging to God: the power of the universe (intently). Man has always considered himself, in your terms, as set apart from nature, so he must feel set apart from nature’s power — and there must be a great division in his dreams between the two. Nuclear energy in fact, then, comes as a dream symbol, and emerges into the world as something to be dealt with.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第844节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "844", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "人梦到他的语言", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2699", "zh": "按照你们的时间尺度，有对你而言如永劫那么长的时间，人在梦境远比他们醒来的时间长。他们睡很长时间，如动物们都是的那样——可以说，醒来只为运动他们的身体、补充体力，后来则为了交配。那的确是个如梦般的世界，但却非常迷人又充满了活力。在其中，做梦的想象力骚乱的玩这新的冒险所惹起的所有可能性：想象各种可能的语言和沟通方式，织出伟大的未来文明之梦的故事，其中充满了它们自己本生的历史——并建造精神性的构架，而因为他们现在和时间联盟了，这架构自动的创造了过去就如创造了未来一样。", "en": "For what would seem to you to be eons, according to your time scale, men were in the dreaming state far more than they were in the waking one. They slept long hours, as did the animals—awakening, so to speak, to exercise their bodies, obtain sustenance, and, later, to mate. It was indeed a dreamlike world, but a highly charming and vital one, in which dreaming imaginations played rambunctiously with all the probabilities entailed in this new venture: imagining the various forms of language and communication possible, spinning great dream tales of future civilizations replete with their own built-in histories—building, because they were now allied with time, mental edifices that automatically created pasts as well as futures.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第893节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "893", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2700", "zh": "到某种程度，这些古老的梦为每一个从事尘世冒险的意识所分享，以致生物和环境一同形成伟大的环境实相。河谷与山峦及其居民，一同把他们自己梦进了存在与共生状态。", "en": "These ancient dreams were shared to some extent by each consciousness that was embarked upon the earthly venture, so that creatures and environment together formed great environmental realities. Valleys and mountains, and their inhabitants, together dreamed themselves into being and coexistence.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第893节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "893", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2703", "zh": "人梦到他的语言。他梦到如何用他的舌头形成字句。在他的梦里，他练习把字符串起来以产生意义，以致最后他能有意识的开始一句话，而并不真的知道它是如何开始的，却有他能够并愿意完成这句子的信心。", "en": "Man dreamed his languages. He dreamed how to use his tongue to form the words. In his dreams he practiced stringing the words together to form their meanings, so that finally he could consciously begin a sentence without actually knowing how it was begun, yet in the faith that he could and would complete it.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第893节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "893", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2704", "zh": "所有的语言都以在梦中所说的语言为其基础，不过，当人变成较少是个做梦者，而较多的沉浸在特定的时刻里时，语言的需要就升起了。因为在梦境他和旁人及其它物种的沟通是即刻的，那么，语言升起以取代那内在的沟通。在人所谓的早期文化——洞穴绘画和宗教——里，有个很大的、在所有之下的根本统一性，因为当人试着将内在知识改换为物质的确实性时，他们全是由那共同的源头所喂养的。", "en": "All languages have as their basis the language that was spoken in dreams. The need for language arose, however, as man became less a dreamer and more immersed in the specifics of space and time, for in the dream state his communications with his fellows and other species was instantaneous. Language arose to take the place of that inner communication, then. There is a great underlying unity in all of man’s so-called early cultures—cave drawings and religions—because they were all fed by that common source, as man tried to transpose inner knowledge into physical actuality.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第893节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "893", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2772", "zh": "个人性最重要的面向是那些主观的特性，它们一方面将一个人与另一个人区分开；而在另一方面，每个都像闪闪发光的心理镶嵌拼图，给那人类由之而出的较大模式分别的、精美的个人版本。以这种方式，每个人的安全感、完整感及灿烂，都升自那宇宙性的基因语言，并且也升自梦内在主观的宇宙性语言。在两者之间有了不起的联系，而两者是一同被说出的。", "en": "The most important aspects of individuality are those subjective characteristics that on the one hand distinguish each person from the other, and that on the other hand are each like sparkling psychological mosaics, giving separate, exquisite individual versions of that larger pattern from which mankind emerges. The security, the integrity, and the brilliance of each individuality rises in these terms from that universal genetic language, and also from the inner subjective universal language of dreams. There are great connections between the two, and both are spoken together.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第911节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "911", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2774", "zh": "对痛苦的追求是不自然的，它是一个古怪的行为模式。许多小孩做白日梦，不只梦到做国王或皇后，或被给予伟大的荣誉，他们也作为生为悲剧人物的白日梦。他们做残酷的死亡的白日梦，他们雀跃于恶毒的后母的故事。事实上，他们尽其所能的想象涉及人类经验的每种情况。到某种程度，成人也做同样的事，他们被涉及了悲剧、悲伤和伟大的戏剧性挣扎的电影或电视所吸引。这是因为，你们活着是你们对人类经验之伟大好奇心的结果。你们活着，因为你们想要参与人类戏剧。", "en": "It is not unnatural. It is an eccentric (pause) behavior pattern. Many children daydream not only of being kings or queens, or given great honors, they also daydream about being tragic figures. They daydream of cruel deaths. They glory in stories of wicked stepmothers. They imagine, in fact, every situation that they can involving human experience. To an extent adults do the same thing. They are drawn to cinema or television dramas that involve tragedies, sorrows, great dramatic struggles. This is because you are alive as the result of your great curiosity for human experience. You are alive because you want to participate in human drama.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第911节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "911", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2775", "zh": "做梦的状态提供了在这些意识系统之间的连接的环节。", "en": "The state of dreaming provides the connecting links between these systems of consciousness.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第911节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "911", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "先天的梦：基因性的那一层", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2777", "zh": "也有我称之为基因性的梦的东西，那是直接被基因的启动所激发的。这些有助于形成及指导意识，当它在出生前存在于任何既定个人里的时候。", "en": "There are also what I will call genetic dreams, which are inspired directly by genetic triggering. These help form and direct consciousness as it exists in any given individual from before birth.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第912节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "912", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2779", "zh": "这种思维是像形成他们自己的磁铁的电流模式。（停顿良久。）在胎儿里已出现了形成观念的能力，而胎儿的确形成观念。那观念形成之精确取向，以及思维模式之精确取向，等待着出生后来自父母及环境的某些具体的触发，但观念成形及思维的过程则已建立了。这个建立发生在基因性的梦里（再次的，全都热切的）。", "en": "Such thoughts are like, now (underline “like, now”), electrical patterns that form their own magnets. (Long pause.) The ability to conceptualize is present in the fetus, and the fetus does conceptualize. The precise orientation of that conceptualizing, and the precise orientation of the thinking patterns, wait for certain physical triggers received from the parents and the environment after birth, but the processes of conceptualization and of thought are already established. This establishment takes place in genetic dreams (again, all intently).", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第912节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "912", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2780", "zh": "等我们一会儿…… 运用语言的能力，是透过精确的取向而基因性的生成的，并且再次的，基因性的以父母的母语实质启动。儿童们早在实际说出这种语言之前，就已在心智上学习了。但再次的，在基因性启动的梦里，婴儿练习语言；可是，在这种婴儿听见父母说话之前，他们是以心电感应沟通的，甚至在婴儿里，基因性的梦也涉及了语言的密码化及诠释。那些梦本身引发带来自己的实相所必须的实质上的构成。", "en": "(Long pause at 9:22.) Give us a moment…. The ability to use language is also genetically built-in, through the precise orientation, again, with the physical triggering of the parents’ native language. Children learn such languages mentally long before they are physically capable of speaking them; but again, in genetically inspired dreams, children—or rather, infants—practice language. Before such infants hear their parents speak, however, they are in telepathic communication, and even in the fetus genetic dreams involve the coding and interpretation of language. Those dreams themselves inspire the physical formations necessary to bring about their own actualizations.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第912节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "912", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2781", "zh": "终你一生，一种或另一种的基因性的梦仍在持续，不论你是否有意识的察觉到。它们在你们所谓的“人的进化”里扮演重要的角色。它们是先前提过的让人去因觅食而迁徙，领他去肥沃之地的那些梦的源头。那些梦也与在物质世界里的生存有极密切的关系，而不论何时，当那生存仿佛受到威胁时，这种梦就会尽可能的升到意识层面。", "en": "Genetic dreams of one kind or another continue throughout your lives, whether or not you are consciously aware of them. They were of prime importance in “man’s evolution,” as you think of it. They were the source of dreams, mentioned earlier, that sent man on migrations after food, that led him toward fertile land. Those dreams are most closely related to survival in physical existence, and whenever that survival seems threatened such dreams arise to consciousness whenever possible.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第912节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "912", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2782", "zh": "基因性的梦是预警饥荒或战争的梦。不过，这种梦在你们自己的时代也常可以被触发，当意识心确信人类的存活受到威胁时——而在这种例子里，那些梦于是真的代表了人的恐惧。那么，过度忧虑能令基因系统混淆，并且是以种种不同的方式。每一个种族的存在都依赖信任，这的确是一种生物性的乐观，在其中每个族类感到能自由而安全的发展其成员的潜能，在存在的自然架构之内。每个族类进入存在时，不只对自己的有效性感觉到一种天然的内在信赖，并且还被自己应付环境的能力的蓬勃生气所实际推动。每个族类知道在生命的结构里，它是独特的适于其位。所有族类的年轻分子，都展示出一种无法抑制的放恣（放恣：指放纵任性）。那放恣是与生俱来的。", "en": "They are the dreams that warn of famines or of wars. Such dreams, however, can also be triggered often, as in your own times, when the conscious mind is convinced that the survival of the species is threatened—and in such cases the dreams then actually represent man’s fears. Overanxiety, then, can confuse the genetic system, and in a variety of ways. The existence of each of the species is dependent upon trust, indeed a biological optimism, in which each species feels the freedom to develop the potentials of its members in relative safety, within the natural frameworks of existence. Each species comes into being not merely feeling a natural built-in trust in its own validity, but is literally propelled by exuberance in its ability to cope with its environment. It knows that it is uniquely suited to its place within life’s framework. The young of all species exhibit an unquenchable rambunctiousness. That rambunctiousness is built in.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第912节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "912", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "内我教肉体走路", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2742", "zh": "在那开始时，梦游一度是非常普遍的经验——远比现在普通——在其中内我真的教肉体走路，因而阻止新冒出的肉体取向的理性问太多的问题，阻碍了身体平顺的自发性移动而挡了它自己的路。", "en": "Sleepwalking was once, in that beginning, a very common experience—far more so than now—in which the inner self actually taught the physical body to walk, and hence presented the newly emerged physically oriented intellect from getting in its own way, asking too many questions that might otherwise impede the body’s smooth spontaneous motion. In the same fashion man is born with an inbuilt propensity for language, and for the communication of symbols through pictures and writing. He spoke first in an automatic fashion that began in his dreams. In a fashion (underlined), you could almost say that he used language before he consciously understood it (quietly). It is not just that he learned by doing, but that the doing did the teaching. Again, lest there be a sharply inquiring intellect, wondering overmuch about how the words were formed or what motions were necessary, his drawing was in the same way automatic. You might almost say—almost—that he used the language (pause) “despite himself.” Therefore, it possessed an almost magical quality, and the “word” was seen as coming directly from God.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第901节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "901", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2756", "zh": "“等到”伊甸园的故事被写成你们圣经故事的时候，整个画面已经被看作是善与恶的观念，这实际上是在人类发展上很久后才出现的。人类心灵的内在转世结构，在人肉体的存活上是非常重要的，因而，他们能梦见前生而记起，比如说，如何走路或说话。他们生而有如何思想的知识，生而有语言的癖好，他们被他们随后忘掉的记忆所导引。", "en": "“By the time” that the Garden of Eden tale reached your biblical stories, the entire picture had already been seen in the light of concepts about good and evil that actually appeared, in those terms, a long time later in man’s development. The inner reincarnational structure of the human psyche is very important in man’s physical survival. Children—change that to “infants”—dream of their past lives, remembering, for example, how to walk and talk. They are born with the knowledge of how to think, with the propensity for language. They are guided by memories that they later forget.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第904节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "904", "only_en": false}]}]}], "reading": [{"id": "q-concept-099-e2074", "zh": "很明显的，以你们的话来说，原子不会梦到猫在追狗，然而（热切的），的确有自物质焦点的“放松”，那可比喻为你们的梦境。", "en": "In your terms, obviously, atoms do not dream of cats chasing dogs, yet (intently) there are indeed “lapses” from physical focus that are analogous to your dreaming state. Give us a moment … In those conditions the atoms pursue their own probable activities, and indeed make astounding calculations, bringing into your actuality the necessary probable actions to insure official life forms. But neither are they limited otherwise, for their other probable directions are also actualized. Period. On different levels in the dream state, then, you are also subjectively aware of other probable realities. Your conscious intent is unconsciously brought into the dreaming condition, and that intent helps you sort the data. (See Note 4.) So, from other streams of actuality you choose those events that you want physically materialized; and you do this according to your beliefs about the nature of reality. A photograph is taken, and you have before you then a picture of an event that in your terms has already happened. In dreams you take many subjective “photographs,” and decide which ones among them you want to materialize in time. To a certain extent, therefore, the dreams are blueprints for your later snapshots.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第699节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "699", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e2300", "zh": "你能学会大量地增加你自己的经验。理论上说，你甚至可对其他的存在有某种程度的觉察。在梦境你可旅行到与自己的实相层面分开的层面。你可以学会以新的方式利用和经验时间。你能从你自己存在的其它部分获取知识，并开发心灵的资源。你能改变你居住的世界以及生活的质量。但当你具有肉体时，你仍得经验出生与死亡，黎明与黄昏，以及每段时刻的私密性，因为这你是所选择的经验。", "en": "You can learn to vastly enrich your own experience. Theoretically, you can even become aware of other existences to some degree. You can travel in the dream state into levels of reality separated from your own. You can learn to use and experience time in new fashions. You can obtain knowledge from other portions of your own being, and tap the psyche’s resources. You can improve the world in which you live, and the quality of life. But while you are physical, you will still experience birth and death, dawn and dusk, and the privacy of the moments, for this is the experience you have chosen.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第759节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "759", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1346", "zh": "这样的梦基本上不一定包含回到遥远的过去在内，因为对细胞而言，万事万物都在当下。这个实相是你当下的存在中一个基本的部分，而且只代表现实的一个维度而已，由于它的本质使然，自我无法承认这个实相。", "en": "Such dreams do not basically imply a return to a distant past, for to the cells all things are present. (Long pause.) This reality is a basic part of your present existence, and simply represents a dimension of actuality that the ego cannot, by its nature, admit.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第290节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "290", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1789", "zh": "可以说，这喜悦延伸到将来，光明也照耀到过去，可能涵盖了比在那一刻能以具体方式表现的更大的范围。再来想象这个人由幻想落入一个出神状态或一个深睡中。（停顿良久）他可能看到对他非常具有象征性的喜悦或兴致勃勃的影像。逻辑上说，它们之间可能没多少关联，但在直觉上这关联是很清楚的。他现在比在幻想状态进入了深得多的精神体验，而可能有一连串的梦，在梦中他能表达他的喜悦而与他人分享。", "en": "The joy stretches out, so to speak, into the future, sheds also its light into the past, and may cover greater areas of expansion than could be shown in physical terms at that moment. Now imagine that our individual from his reverie falls either into a trance state or into a deep sleep. (Long pause.) He may see images that are highly symbolic to him of joy or exuberance. Logically there may be little connection between them, but intuitively the connections are clear. He now enters into his mental experiences far more deeply than in the reverie state, and may have a series of dream episodes in which he is able to express his joy and share it with others.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第571节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "571", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0832", "zh": "这样的了解让后续的梦。也就是第二个梦变得可能。如果没有第一个梦，接下来的梦也不可能发生。第二个梦是一个扩展的梦。我们将会从两个层面来讨论它。最有意义的层面是，很多的房间代表了很多心理领域的发展，无限的可能性持续的打开；但可能性奠基于之前的人生经验，在这个梦里有很多转世方面的资料，所有这些都强化了鲁柏人格上关于健康的一面。", "en": "This realization made the following dream, the second dream, possible. But the following dream would not have been possible without the first. The second dream is a dream of expansion. We will discuss it from two levels. The most meaningful level was one in which the many rooms represented psychic areas of development, endless possibilities that continually opened; but possibilities which were based on previous life experiences, and there are many aspects of reincarnational data in this dream, all reinforcing the healthy aspects of Ruburt's personality.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第199节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "199", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0978", "zh": "在之前的梦里，你很确定你有能力从恐惧的处境下存活下来。在这个梦里，你面临着一个可能性，你可能没那么容易可以存活下来。鲁柏在这个插曲里，因为现在你担心自己的反应会对他的情况有所影响。", "en": "In the earlier dreams you were certain of your capacity to survive the situation which you feared. In this dream you faced the possibility that you would not survive so easily. Ruburt is in this episode because now you worry about the effect of your own reaction upon his condition in general.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第216节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "216", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e1023", "zh": "现在。我们确实应该在鲁柏不预期的情况下，及时讨论他的梦。现在我想要把一些关于倒置时间和可能性事件的内容加进我们的讨论里。", "en": "Now. We shall indeed discuss Ruburts dream in good time, when he is not expecting it. For now, I would like to add somewhat to our discussion concerning inverted time and probable events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第233节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "233", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0336", "zh": "晚晚的梦是来自海伦的通讯。她是鲁柏母亲的朋友。这个讯息被导向鲁柏。他不是从他母亲那里接收到它。那个信封、那个黑色信封，显然是一个象征，但它确实让鲁柏能够看见传送讯息的女人叫什么名字。这个梦的意义对他而言，只有在那份简单资料的感知中是清楚的，也就是那个黑色信封，以及左下方的寄件人姓名，但是他想不起来，信封上有这则通讯的接收方，他的名字。", "en": "Last nights dream was a communication from the Helen who was the friend of Ruburt's mother. The message was directed to Ruburt. He did not pick it up from his mother. The envelope, the black envelope, was obviously a symbol, but it did enable Ruburt to see the name of the woman who was sending the message. And the import of the dream was clear to him merely in the perception of that simple data, the black envelope, with the return name in the left hand corner, and though he does not recall it, his name on the envelope as the person to whom the communication was sent.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第105节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "105", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0260", "zh": "因为现在的情况是，这个人在你当前的生活中只是一个少年，所以这个梦就改变了层次。那条船变成了一个男性的象征。第一个象征围绕着鲁柏的梦被建立起来，后者涉及一个女性的象征；也就是说，现在的鲁柏把那条船解读为“tub”，因此是洗衣机，漏水的船变成漏水的洗衣机。", "en": "Because of the present situation, where the man in your present life is a mere lad, the dream then changed levels. The vessel becomes a masculine symbol. The first symbol was built around Ruburt’s dreams, which involved a female symbol; that is, the present Ruburt interpreted vessel as tub, hence washing machine, the leaking vessel becoming a leaking washing machine.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第94节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "94", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0158", "zh": "那一组日子的梦顺序涉及感知，首先是鲁柏在前世的实际死亡，接着同一天晚上往回跳到更早以前的事件。整个顺序的目的是安慰，而且就整体的性质而言，和人格对逐渐到来的冬天以及它暗示的象征性死亡升起的内在反应有关。", "en": "The dream sequence for that group of days involved perception, first, of Ruburt’s actual previous death, and then leaped that same evening back furl her to much earlier events. The purpose of the whole sequence was reassuring, and in overall nature had to do with the personality’s inner reaction to the season of an approaching winter and the ever symbolic death that it suggests.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第87节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "87", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-099-e0159", "zh": "所以一方面，鲁柏的心灵期盼着冬天和它的象征意义，而让你卷入他关切的事情当中。这个梦说鲁柏死了一次但又活过来，还说就像你有一次从一艘漏水的老旧船只死里逃生一样，基本的自己们永远会存活下来。", "en": "On one level then Ruburt was psychically anticipating winter with its symbolism, and he involved you in his concern. The dream said that Ruburt died once and yet lived again, and that as you survived a leaky old ship at one occasion, so would the basic selves always survive.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第87节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "87", "only_en": false}], "books": [{"name": "早期课第3册", "count": 34}, {"name": "梦、进化与价值完成", "count": 32}, {"name": "早期课第4册", "count": 20}, {"name": "早期课第5册", "count": 16}, {"name": "早期课第6册", "count": 16}, {"name": "心灵的本质", "count": 12}, {"name": "早期课第7册", "count": 11}, {"name": "未知的实相卷二", "count": 8}, {"name": "灵魂永生", "count": 8}, {"name": "时空之外", "count": 7}, {"name": "早期课第9册", "count": 5}, {"name": "早期课第1册", "count": 4}, {"name": "个人与群体事件的本质", "count": 4}, {"name": "个人实相的本质", "count": 4}, {"name": "早期课第2册", "count": 3}, {"name": "健康之道", "count": 2}, {"name": "未知的实相卷一", "count": 2}, {"name": "早期课第8册", "count": 1}, {"name": "神奇之道", "count": 1}], "span": [1963, 1984], "stats": {"blocks": 11, "quotes": 190, "reading": 11, "reading_tagged": 0, "tagged": 0, "only_en": 0}};
